00:30:12.834 --> 00:30:16.834
major problem, which is the market rates in this

00:30:16.834 --> 00:30:20.500
city are out of control. Those are contributing to

00:30:21.500 --> 00:30:25.166
people having high taxes in houses that they

00:30:25.200 --> 00:30:28.967
bought that are now worth double, and they're paying taxes for

00:30:29.100 --> 00:30:33.066
that house. The other piece of it is that we have not

00:30:33.100 --> 00:30:37.066
put in the work to build in housing to spread

00:30:37.100 --> 00:30:40.800
that tax base across more people.And I think it's

00:30:41.133 --> 00:30:45.066
weird that our solution is to fire our public

00:30:45.133 --> 00:30:49.066
employees, and increase the unemployment rate, and lower

00:30:49.166 --> 00:30:51.400
the number of taxes to try to do this.

00:30:51.867 --> 00:30:53.033
I've heard that our

00:30:54.367 --> 00:30:58.200
student council is going to... Or sorry, our school board is trying

00:30:58.300 --> 00:31:00.166
to say this isn't going to happen again.

00:31:00.634 --> 00:31:03.800
But we don't have a forward-thinking policy.

00:31:05.200 --> 00:31:08.066
What's South Portland going to look like in five years?

00:31:08.567 --> 00:31:12.100
Where's the economic growth? Where's the radical

00:31:12.200 --> 00:31:15.867
revenue generation that we'd like

00:31:16.266 --> 00:31:17.800
to make our city prosperous?

00:31:18.567 --> 00:31:22.333
I don't hear on any of these city councils

00:31:22.367 --> 00:31:25.934
since I heard, is there any organization or business

00:31:26.066 --> 00:31:29.767
currently getting less than the residential mill tax rate?

00:31:30.300 --> 00:31:33.967
Are there any that are currently getting tax breaks that maybe we can ask

00:31:34.033 --> 00:31:36.800
them to contribute to that for one more year?

00:31:37.300 --> 00:31:39.433
Lower the average number of taxes here.

00:31:40.066 --> 00:31:44.000
I am more than happy as a citizen to contribute more in taxes if

00:31:44.066 --> 00:31:47.967
that means that lowers the tax base for those

00:31:48.066 --> 00:31:51.667
most vulnerable in our city. Why are we having seniors

00:31:52.000 --> 00:31:54.867
that are living in half a million dollar homes?

00:31:55.300 --> 00:31:58.000
Because we haven't put the investment into housing.

00:31:58.400 --> 00:32:00.967
We haven't asked people to pay their fair share.

00:32:01.767 --> 00:32:03.066
This is where we're at now.

00:32:03.166 --> 00:32:03.600
30 seconds.

00:32:03.634 --> 00:32:05.867
And we're going to be here again next year.

00:32:06.533 --> 00:32:10.233
And I appreciate Scott. Scott's presentation last week was

00:32:10.266 --> 00:32:13.767
incredible, but it also showed that if we're going to

00:32:13.834 --> 00:32:17.567
start raising more revenue, we also have to think

00:32:17.667 --> 00:32:21.200
about our mill rate and how much funding we get from the

00:32:21.300 --> 00:32:25.033
state. So that's my time. I really want to ask,

00:32:25.800 --> 00:32:29.567
what are some of those policies? Can we do a policy of radical

00:32:29.600 --> 00:32:33.500
revenue generation? Can we have a progressive property tax rate

00:32:33.900 --> 00:32:37.333
so that some people like myself can pay more to save our

00:32:38.166 --> 00:32:41.834
other citizens less money? So those are my big

00:32:41.934 --> 00:32:45.567
questions, and that's my spiel, but that's what I wanted to ask of the council.

00:32:46.166 --> 00:32:47.300
Thank you very much.

00:32:48.634 --> 00:32:50.567
Someone else with public comment? Yes.

00:32:51.500 --> 00:32:54.100
Hi, my name's Jack Bowler, and I'm a South Portland resident.

00:32:54.467 --> 00:32:58.467
This situation has made one thing clear, the school department cannot solve this

00:32:58.533 --> 00:33:02.400
alone, and the community should not be expected to carry it without a

00:33:02.467 --> 00:33:06.100
clear path forward. What feels especially concerning

00:33:07.100 --> 00:33:08.333
is the deep distrust

00:33:09.533 --> 00:33:13.467
many in the community are feeling towards the schools right now, and when

00:33:13.567 --> 00:33:17.500
trust in our schools starts to erode, trust in our city can erode with it.

00:33:18.734 --> 00:33:22.634
These institutions are deeply connected, and confidence in one

00:33:22.700 --> 00:33:25.300
inevitably shapes confidence in another.

00:33:26.700 --> 00:33:30.100
I'm not sure how involved the city is meant to be in an issue like this,

00:33:30.667 --> 00:33:32.000
but I would sincerely hope

00:33:32.834 --> 00:33:36.500
this is a moment where city leadership steps up and gets involved.

00:33:36.900 --> 00:33:38.900
People are not asking for perfection.

00:33:39.100 --> 00:33:42.066
They are asking for a plan, a clear, coordinated

00:33:42.200 --> 00:33:45.967
path forward between the city and the school department, that

00:33:46.000 --> 00:33:49.934
explains the options, the trade-offs, and where we are actually

00:33:50.033 --> 00:33:53.900
going. Right now, it feels like we are reacting instead of

00:33:53.967 --> 00:33:56.600
being led. This is a moment for leadership.

00:33:56.934 --> 00:33:58.066
Thanks for what you guys all do.

00:33:58.867 --> 00:34:01.500
Thank you very much. Next.

00:34:03.066 --> 00:34:06.900
I'm sure a lot of you are here to make public comments, so please don't

00:34:06.967 --> 00:34:07.700
be shy.

00:34:10.133 --> 00:34:13.867
Chelsea Bian. I live on Margaret Street, and I'll just be brief to say

00:34:14.166 --> 00:34:14.433
I

00:34:15.834 --> 00:34:18.166
generally support the request by the school department.

00:34:19.433 --> 00:34:23.133
And I think it is a very prudent

00:34:23.333 --> 00:34:26.934
request that this money is not adding

00:34:27.400 --> 00:34:31.300
additional spending that will add to the operating budget for next

00:34:31.467 --> 00:34:33.567
year, but is really trying to give

00:34:33.634 --> 00:34:37.166
the school department a more stable budget

00:34:37.667 --> 00:34:38.467
moving forward.

00:34:39.533 --> 00:34:43.166
It's been a painful few years, and

00:34:43.900 --> 00:34:47.734
I appreciate that this is trying to lessen the pain in the

00:34:47.767 --> 00:34:48.066
future.

00:34:49.500 --> 00:34:51.767
Thank you very much. Thank you.

00:34:52.700 --> 00:34:53.200
Good to hear.

00:34:54.266 --> 00:34:54.667
All right.

00:34:56.867 --> 00:34:57.900
Come on. There.

00:34:58.500 --> 00:35:00.800
Hi. Jenny Lupica. I live on Bower Street.

00:35:00.867 --> 00:35:04.734
I just wanted to say really quickly, because it seems like people have a few

00:35:04.934 --> 00:35:08.900
questions about what will happen next year and kind

00:35:08.967 --> 00:35:12.934
of what the future holds. And I wanted to bring up some of the words that have been

00:35:12.967 --> 00:35:14.867
talked about with this particular budget.

00:35:15.400 --> 00:35:18.033
Daniel Feller, who I don't know if he's here, but he has talked about this being a

00:35:18.100 --> 00:35:21.934
budget crisis. Just tonight, Abigail had said it's really been a

00:35:21.967 --> 00:35:25.100
difficult and painful process. I've heard our

00:35:25.934 --> 00:35:26.400
previous

00:35:27.500 --> 00:35:30.166
school board members calling it a fairly unusual circumstance.

00:35:30.600 --> 00:35:32.767
And I think it's just

00:35:34.100 --> 00:35:37.700
important that we all remember this isn't going to be a request that the school

00:35:37.767 --> 00:35:39.967
board comes to you guys with every single year.

00:35:40.834 --> 00:35:44.600
We're asking for the SROs and the snow removal for

00:35:44.600 --> 00:35:48.533
$360,000, which wouldn't be... I get you saying

00:35:48.567 --> 00:35:51.867
it's like an additional percent, but it'd be coming out of your budget

00:35:52.634 --> 00:35:56.500
instead of adding it to ours, so it wouldn't be an additional percentage that

00:35:57.166 --> 00:36:00.700
citizens would be paying, which I think is really important to consider. Correct?

00:36:01.367 --> 00:36:04.700
Mm-hmm. And I think anything you guys can do to help

00:36:05.700 --> 00:36:09.433
make this situation any less painful based on what's already happened would be

00:36:09.734 --> 00:36:13.567
so appreciated, because it's not quite over yet, but it's getting there.

00:36:14.000 --> 00:36:14.266
Thank you.

00:36:15.400 --> 00:36:17.367
Thank you again. Thank you.

00:36:19.367 --> 00:36:19.367
Thanks.

00:36:21.400 --> 00:36:23.100
Hi, everybody. Eleni Richardson.

00:36:24.700 --> 00:36:26.467
I'm a school board member, but I'm speaking

00:36:27.967 --> 00:36:29.500
as a very tired mom tonight.

00:36:31.634 --> 00:36:35.600
I've emailed this council a couple of times about the

00:36:35.667 --> 00:36:37.100
research that I've been doing

00:36:38.000 --> 00:36:40.600
with other municipalities, and

00:36:41.867 --> 00:36:42.834
local municipalities

00:36:42.900 --> 00:36:46.600
about-How does

00:36:46.734 --> 00:36:48.934
one deal with this kind of an issue?

00:36:49.133 --> 00:36:51.500
I'm a lawyer, I'm a problem solver, and

00:36:52.667 --> 00:36:55.734
the math hasn't been mathing for us over with the school budget.

00:36:56.467 --> 00:36:56.700
And,

00:36:58.367 --> 00:36:59.100
I've been trying to

00:37:00.066 --> 00:37:03.567
just explore creative ways to kind of

00:37:04.333 --> 00:37:08.233
soften the blow of the intensity of the changes that

00:37:08.266 --> 00:37:11.533
the school department is undergoing, or will be undergoing.

00:37:13.734 --> 00:37:13.934
So,

00:37:14.800 --> 00:37:18.600
and I haven't stress tested this, but as a lawyer, I look for precedent,

00:37:19.166 --> 00:37:23.033
and precedent means that it's worked in the past, or

00:37:23.066 --> 00:37:25.300
that there's a model that we can follow.

00:37:25.400 --> 00:37:28.533
And, I sent you all a joint resolution

00:37:28.700 --> 00:37:32.533
between the Portland City Council and the Portland School Board back

00:37:32.567 --> 00:37:33.467
in 2008,

00:37:34.667 --> 00:37:38.433
kind of addressing a partnership, if you will, for the

00:37:38.467 --> 00:37:41.033
creation of a sustainability plan.

00:37:41.734 --> 00:37:45.300
I also sent you the sustainability plan that kind of

00:37:45.333 --> 00:37:49.233
explained how that partnership worked, and there was sort

00:37:49.266 --> 00:37:52.367
of a sharing of resources, if you will.

00:37:54.166 --> 00:37:56.967
And the city had an opportunity to

00:37:58.066 --> 00:38:02.033
work with the school and kind of stress test some of the issues that the school

00:38:02.066 --> 00:38:03.066
department had been having.

00:38:05.166 --> 00:38:08.367
I was going to call you Congressman Walker, but that's not right. Councilor.

00:38:08.734 --> 00:38:10.266
But it has such a ring.

00:38:11.767 --> 00:38:12.133
You've been-

00:38:12.233 --> 00:38:12.600
Watch out.

00:38:12.734 --> 00:38:13.533
You've had a raise.

00:38:15.233 --> 00:38:18.900
Councilor Walker's questions about how is this not going to happen again and

00:38:19.433 --> 00:38:22.133
kind of... Those are really valid questions.

00:38:22.166 --> 00:38:25.567
So maybe the city can come down the street

00:38:25.634 --> 00:38:29.367
and sit with Abigail and her team and

00:38:32.166 --> 00:38:36.066
help in some way. I don't know. But I'm

00:38:36.100 --> 00:38:39.467
not trying to do your job. I'm not trying to offer anything that is

00:38:39.500 --> 00:38:42.567
inappropriate or not transparent to taxpayers.

00:38:43.133 --> 00:38:47.033
I'm simply trying to find other ways.

00:38:47.834 --> 00:38:51.700
We've already, as a board, asked for your blessing

00:38:51.767 --> 00:38:53.967
on an additional tax increase.

00:38:55.667 --> 00:38:57.767
Councilor Pride, you're right. This budget

00:38:58.934 --> 00:39:01.266
has presented a number of issues for the public.

00:39:02.533 --> 00:39:06.400
My hope is last night's decision to potentially pause on

00:39:06.433 --> 00:39:09.867
reconfiguration allows taxpayers to

00:39:10.367 --> 00:39:14.000
think just about the school budget right now, given that

00:39:14.467 --> 00:39:18.467
the decision we made last night does show that we are listening to the community,

00:39:18.467 --> 00:39:22.100
and most importantly, our classroom teachers, which I think is what

00:39:22.133 --> 00:39:24.934
potentially swayed other people to change their mind.

00:39:24.967 --> 00:39:25.233
Time.

00:39:26.467 --> 00:39:30.333
But anyway, I just want to implore you to please open your minds

00:39:31.100 --> 00:39:32.333
and help. Thank you.

00:39:33.333 --> 00:39:35.333
Hold on for just one moment. Just a quick...

00:39:36.767 --> 00:39:40.667
Generally, when someone asks a question, the city manager answers it.

00:39:40.834 --> 00:39:44.333
And I am intuiting that your

00:39:44.467 --> 00:39:46.667
question might have been,

00:39:48.033 --> 00:39:51.767
what kind of reaction or what kind of process would there be

00:39:51.867 --> 00:39:54.266
to discussing this material you've sent?

00:39:54.934 --> 00:39:55.233
Correct.

00:39:55.500 --> 00:39:55.867
All right.

00:39:56.066 --> 00:39:57.967
Sure. Yeah. Thank you for putting...

00:40:00.934 --> 00:40:02.967
All right. Yeah. What a question. Wow.

00:40:03.133 --> 00:40:03.333
Thank you.

00:40:03.400 --> 00:40:03.867
Thank you.

00:40:04.200 --> 00:40:06.166
I should have posited a question. Thank you for-

00:40:06.734 --> 00:40:07.000
All right

00:40:07.133 --> 00:40:07.333
... doing it for me.

00:40:07.333 --> 00:40:08.300
Thanks very much.

00:40:08.333 --> 00:40:08.667
Thank you.

00:40:10.133 --> 00:40:12.000
More public comment, please.

00:40:14.634 --> 00:40:17.767
Hey, everybody. I'm Tyler Smith. I'm also on the school board.

00:40:18.467 --> 00:40:22.233
I just wanted to quickly voice my support for the work that

00:40:22.767 --> 00:40:26.634
the school administration, Abigail, Joe, and everybody else has done

00:40:27.367 --> 00:40:31.033
on this budget. I'm new to the school board and new to the city and

00:40:31.433 --> 00:40:32.133
this school.

00:40:33.166 --> 00:40:35.467
In particular, I come with a background in schools.

00:40:36.567 --> 00:40:40.533
I worked for a grant-making organization that did technical assistance

00:40:40.667 --> 00:40:43.834
for schools with their budgets and how to

00:40:45.166 --> 00:40:48.133
make things work, especially with declining enrollment.

00:40:49.800 --> 00:40:51.000
Some context for the

00:40:51.934 --> 00:40:55.734
city council is that when federal COVID funds went

00:40:55.800 --> 00:40:59.700
out at this point four or five years ago, one of the main things that

00:40:59.767 --> 00:41:03.634
we were talking with school leaders about is not spending our funds

00:41:03.734 --> 00:41:06.133
on positions, because that money runs out.

00:41:06.867 --> 00:41:10.834
South Portland dealt with a very unique issue, which is that they

00:41:10.900 --> 00:41:14.734
also accepted a large number of asylum seekers at the same time,

00:41:14.767 --> 00:41:17.567
and so chose to spend almost all of their funds on positions,

00:41:19.200 --> 00:41:21.634
which is what got us to this position

00:41:22.467 --> 00:41:23.967
where we are right now, primarily.

00:41:24.333 --> 00:41:27.967
There's obviously been a lot of turnover in terms of

00:41:28.433 --> 00:41:32.166
the director of finance, and that has created its own set of problems, but

00:41:32.467 --> 00:41:34.000
I think those two things are working together.

00:41:34.066 --> 00:41:36.900
And I think the administration, the work they've done over the last

00:41:37.900 --> 00:41:41.100
five, six, eight months to get us back on track

00:41:41.967 --> 00:41:45.533
and put a budget together that's realistic and that will set us up for the

00:41:45.567 --> 00:41:45.967
future

00:41:47.066 --> 00:41:47.967
is commendable.

00:41:49.000 --> 00:41:52.700
And I'll also say that we're definitely not out of the woods after this year.

00:41:52.767 --> 00:41:56.266
Abigail was discussing the FY28 budget, and our current

00:41:56.333 --> 00:42:00.166
roll-forward projections put us at 6%, pretty much on the dot.

00:42:00.934 --> 00:42:02.834
And that's with a lot of question marks about

00:42:03.934 --> 00:42:07.867
fuel, electricity, the kinds of operational costs that are going to come up in the

00:42:07.900 --> 00:42:09.867
next year that are really big question marks.

00:42:10.967 --> 00:42:14.834
And so, I implore you guys to take this budget

00:42:14.900 --> 00:42:16.367
and accept it, and

00:42:17.266 --> 00:42:19.967
2%, I think, would be great for building a contingency.

00:42:21.066 --> 00:42:22.934
But we're also not going to be out of the woods with this.

00:42:23.033 --> 00:42:26.934
So, to set expectations that next year will

00:42:26.934 --> 00:42:29.433
not be this hard, but it may be hard.

00:42:29.634 --> 00:42:32.500
And so we have to, I think, look forward to a

00:42:32.533 --> 00:42:36.367
larger term after this year, really thinking about the

00:42:36.400 --> 00:42:39.867
next five, 10 years, and how this city can make our schools work long term.

00:42:40.166 --> 00:42:40.867
So, thank you.

00:42:43.900 --> 00:42:46.266
Thank you very much. Other public comment?

00:42:49.533 --> 00:42:50.033
Thank you.

00:42:50.133 --> 00:42:51.567
Sometimes better than now, I guess.

00:42:53.700 --> 00:42:57.000
Ed Cobb, Romano Road. I spoke last night at the school board meeting for the first

00:42:57.066 --> 00:42:58.934
time ever. I've also watched the meetings,

00:43:00.133 --> 00:43:03.433
and I will say, like what I said last night, it was a lot of contentious

00:43:04.767 --> 00:43:08.266
stuff, and I don't envy being on the school board,

00:43:08.400 --> 00:43:12.300
and getting phone calls, and threatening

00:43:12.300 --> 00:43:14.667
that I don't know what I'm doing, and all kinds of stuff like that.

00:43:14.700 --> 00:43:16.200
So I guess thank God I'm not on the school board.

00:43:16.834 --> 00:43:19.300
But I will say part of the issue, I think is...

00:43:20.500 --> 00:43:22.834
And to me, it's the most ridiculous thing ever.

00:43:23.367 --> 00:43:25.233
We vote on a school budget in June.

00:43:26.033 --> 00:43:29.700
Roughly speaking, 11% to 12% of the people show up to vote on that school board out

00:43:29.734 --> 00:43:30.767
of the whole population.

00:43:32.433 --> 00:43:35.533
We don't really know, if I'm understanding what you're saying correctly,

00:43:36.266 --> 00:43:36.634
how

00:43:37.967 --> 00:43:40.467
much spending we may be looking at coming up.

00:43:40.667 --> 00:43:42.800
It's just something you don't know. It's an unknown.

00:43:43.100 --> 00:43:46.100
And for the taxpayer, what's an unknown is we don't know what our new assessed

00:43:46.133 --> 00:43:49.200
value's going to be. And I reached out to Mr.

00:43:49.367 --> 00:43:51.900
Matthews during the last assessed value, and I will say Mr.

00:43:52.033 --> 00:43:54.200
Morelli called me and said, "Hey, do you want to sit down?" I go, "I don't want to

00:43:54.233 --> 00:43:57.133
have to sit down." But regardless, his assessed value went up

00:43:57.166 --> 00:44:00.867
$36,000 last year. My assessed value for the

00:44:00.934 --> 00:44:04.767
land that I haven't done anything with went up $3,600 out of nowhere,

00:44:04.834 --> 00:44:05.834
really, in my mind.

00:44:06.734 --> 00:44:09.734
The cheapest property, stick-built property in this town, as I mentioned last

00:44:09.767 --> 00:44:13.100
night, is $399,000. It was listed three days ago.

00:44:13.467 --> 00:44:16.500
It's 650 square feet, and the property taxes are

00:44:16.600 --> 00:44:20.533
$4,300 or so annually on a 650 square

00:44:20.567 --> 00:44:21.100
feet building.

00:44:22.634 --> 00:44:26.467
This is unsustainable. And my thought would be, in all

00:44:26.500 --> 00:44:30.433
honesty, if possible, would be take last year's budget for the

00:44:30.467 --> 00:44:33.066
city and for the school department, carry it over.

00:44:33.734 --> 00:44:36.100
Let's know some of those unknowns around September.

00:44:36.667 --> 00:44:40.667
Let's have the public vote on it in November when we all sort of know where

00:44:40.667 --> 00:44:43.800
we're really going to be at. The public will know where their assessed value will

00:44:43.834 --> 00:44:46.467
be. The school probably will have a better idea of where they

00:44:46.500 --> 00:44:50.367
will be. And ultimately, I think it's a fairer

00:44:50.900 --> 00:44:51.333
system.

00:44:52.300 --> 00:44:56.133
I don't think that there's a law that says we have to have the

00:44:56.166 --> 00:44:58.300
budget passed by June. I could be wrong.

00:44:59.100 --> 00:45:01.734
But I think we could carry over the existing budgets.

00:45:02.533 --> 00:45:02.700
Let's

00:45:03.800 --> 00:45:07.500
see where we really all are at and have a vote in November,

00:45:07.734 --> 00:45:10.433
where I think we get more people from the public coming out to vote for it.

00:45:10.800 --> 00:45:14.700
I think people see June elections, and people are off doing stuff with their kids.

00:45:15.200 --> 00:45:17.967
I'm going to be off doing stuff with my kid, who's going to get out of college here

00:45:18.000 --> 00:45:18.767
pretty soon. So

00:45:19.700 --> 00:45:22.734
that's my first thought. I guess the other thing I would mention is

00:45:23.800 --> 00:45:25.100
I certainly would like to see-

00:45:25.500 --> 00:45:26.000
30 seconds

00:45:26.033 --> 00:45:26.734
... the teachers

00:45:27.834 --> 00:45:31.667
be more public-facing, meaning I'd like to see more teachers, and I'd like to see

00:45:31.734 --> 00:45:33.700
more administration cuts

00:45:34.567 --> 00:45:37.300
or salary freezes in the administration level.

00:45:37.600 --> 00:45:41.233
Not trying to take money away from teachers, but I think that's who talks to those

00:45:41.300 --> 00:45:44.600
students every day, not the administrators in their back offices.

00:45:44.634 --> 00:45:45.800
I'm not saying they don't work hard.

00:45:46.033 --> 00:45:49.400
I'm just saying they don't face the students on a daily basis. Thanks so much.

00:45:50.500 --> 00:45:51.634
Thank you very much.

00:45:52.533 --> 00:45:54.133
Additional public comment?

00:46:01.200 --> 00:46:02.066
Excuse me.

00:46:05.333 --> 00:46:09.100
Lauren Shapiro, Deek Street. I want to acknowledge the school

00:46:09.166 --> 00:46:11.967
board and leadership for all of their effort.

00:46:12.600 --> 00:46:14.600
It was an impossible budget to work with.

00:46:15.266 --> 00:46:19.166
We see you, and we appreciate you and all that you've done throughout this

00:46:19.200 --> 00:46:20.300
difficult process.

00:46:21.467 --> 00:46:24.533
I'm here to repeat the same message you've all heard before.

00:46:25.734 --> 00:46:29.266
I again want to call out very real inequity that exists in our

00:46:29.300 --> 00:46:29.834
community.

00:46:30.834 --> 00:46:34.800
Industrial concentrations have shaped housing, property values,

00:46:34.800 --> 00:46:38.467
and environmental conditions, placing an unequal burden on certain

00:46:38.533 --> 00:46:39.233
neighborhoods.

00:46:40.166 --> 00:46:44.066
These realities must be acknowledged and addressed in future planning and policy

00:46:44.100 --> 00:46:47.567
decisions to protect health, expand opportunities, and help bridge

00:46:47.600 --> 00:46:49.066
inequities in the school system.

00:46:50.333 --> 00:46:54.333
As we know, inequity starts with housing, and addressing it at the root is

00:46:54.333 --> 00:46:58.266
essential to creating lasting fairness and opportunity for all children and

00:46:58.367 --> 00:46:59.000
families.

00:47:00.133 --> 00:47:04.033
It's critical to note that the tank farms which sprawl across our city and

00:47:04.133 --> 00:47:07.500
infiltrate our neighborhoods have been systematically undervalued for

00:47:07.533 --> 00:47:08.133
decades.

00:47:09.200 --> 00:47:13.100
While residents have faced continual and substantial increases

00:47:13.100 --> 00:47:17.066
in property values, the tank farms have never and continue to

00:47:17.066 --> 00:47:21.000
avoid their fair share of contribution to the rising costs of our city,

00:47:21.834 --> 00:47:25.333
all while their emissions harm the health and wellbeing of our

00:47:25.367 --> 00:47:29.266
residents. Based on 2021 assessor's data, even

00:47:29.300 --> 00:47:32.634
before the recent revaluation that further widens the gap,

00:47:33.233 --> 00:47:37.166
tank values per acre were one-seventh of residential and industrial

00:47:37.233 --> 00:47:39.900
warehouse one-fifth of residential.

00:47:39.934 --> 00:47:43.500
Why do we continue to subsidize these parcels at the expense of our

00:47:43.533 --> 00:47:47.500
community when we are in such dire need for funding our city

00:47:47.533 --> 00:47:48.634
and school needs?

00:47:49.500 --> 00:47:52.567
It is inequitable and unsustainable.

00:47:52.700 --> 00:47:56.500
Fairly assessing these parcels would generate significant revenue for our

00:47:56.600 --> 00:48:00.100
schools, supporting staff, programs, and resources that

00:48:00.200 --> 00:48:03.500
benefit every child. It's long overdue.

00:48:03.900 --> 00:48:05.700
Please make it happen. Thank you.

00:48:06.200 --> 00:48:09.500
Thank you very much. Additional public comment?

00:48:10.567 --> 00:48:12.767
If there isn't any, I'm going to... Okay.

00:48:14.433 --> 00:48:16.500
I'm not threatening anyone with this, but-

00:48:18.600 --> 00:48:20.800
Claire Holman. I'm also a member of the school board.

00:48:22.000 --> 00:48:24.600
A couple of things. I want to say that

00:48:25.500 --> 00:48:29.200
I'm thankful to our budget

00:48:29.233 --> 00:48:33.100
director, Abigail Kirchin, for helping to create this budget

00:48:33.233 --> 00:48:37.033
and for the rest of our administration. I do support the budget.

00:48:37.033 --> 00:48:40.533
It's been a very difficult season, as I know you've all heard.

00:48:42.600 --> 00:48:42.934
And

00:48:43.900 --> 00:48:46.467
I hope that it would be nice if we can get some help.

00:48:46.934 --> 00:48:50.900
I am concerned about burden on taxpayers. It's something we need to think about.

00:48:51.734 --> 00:48:55.533
I've heard some interesting ideas right now and some other interesting ideas, and I

00:48:55.567 --> 00:48:59.133
want to voice once again that, umFunding our

00:48:59.233 --> 00:49:03.066
cities only with property taxes is not a great

00:49:03.166 --> 00:49:05.133
path forward for us for several reasons.

00:49:05.533 --> 00:49:09.266
One of the reasons is the development pattern that it's going to force upon us.

00:49:09.600 --> 00:49:11.667
The second is that it's inequitable.

00:49:12.033 --> 00:49:15.333
Property taxes are regressive inherently.

00:49:15.867 --> 00:49:19.800
So, Scott Morelli once said something about a possible sales tax.

00:49:20.100 --> 00:49:23.300
Well, that's also regressive, but at least some tourists and stuff would be paying

00:49:23.333 --> 00:49:27.200
it, unlike property taxes. And there are other types of things we could

00:49:27.233 --> 00:49:31.033
consider doing. I really think it's not a healthy path for us to always

00:49:31.066 --> 00:49:34.700
depend on property tax. Well, I don't know, why are we doing

00:49:34.767 --> 00:49:37.734
that? It's painful, and it's risky for people.

00:49:38.433 --> 00:49:40.800
So, and then I want to say that

00:49:43.934 --> 00:49:47.834
the idea that we may contribute, what we make, if we go to 2%,

00:49:47.900 --> 00:49:49.133
for example, 2% more,

00:49:50.033 --> 00:49:53.333
for example, that that could help fund our contingency plan, that's very important.

00:49:54.133 --> 00:49:56.266
We have lost some very valuable positions.

00:49:56.867 --> 00:49:57.100
And

00:49:58.333 --> 00:50:01.834
we're hoping that there are some other funds that seem to be on the horizon, like

00:50:01.900 --> 00:50:03.500
for our insurance rate not being as high.

00:50:03.800 --> 00:50:05.400
We're hoping there's going to be some relief there.

00:50:05.400 --> 00:50:08.033
But it's painful to lose some of the people we have lost.

00:50:08.433 --> 00:50:10.200
It's hard enough to close a school.

00:50:10.266 --> 00:50:10.500
And

00:50:11.600 --> 00:50:14.300
I know some people may feel, well, why do you need such fancy schools?

00:50:14.333 --> 00:50:17.567
They're not fancy, but what we do want is really good schools.

00:50:17.934 --> 00:50:21.100
And that's something that we all value, and because

00:50:22.367 --> 00:50:25.300
our children are going to be running this place in a while, and we want to make

00:50:25.333 --> 00:50:27.433
sure they're well-equipped to do so. Thank you.

00:50:28.600 --> 00:50:29.667
Thank you very much.

00:50:30.600 --> 00:50:32.834
Oh, in just over two minutes even.

00:50:34.800 --> 00:50:35.100
All right.

00:50:36.066 --> 00:50:36.233
One

00:50:37.233 --> 00:50:38.467
minute.

00:50:38.500 --> 00:50:38.700
Two.

00:50:43.033 --> 00:50:46.834
Rosemary DeAngelis, Pleasantdale, member of the school board also.

00:50:47.300 --> 00:50:51.100
I just want to reiterate what it is that the

00:50:51.233 --> 00:50:54.867
school board voted last night, because that's the only thing

00:50:55.367 --> 00:50:59.000
that we all agreed on, was asking for a

00:50:59.200 --> 00:51:00.467
1% increase,

00:51:01.300 --> 00:51:03.934
which was going from 6% to 7%,

00:51:04.533 --> 00:51:08.533
and to ask for the city to fund

00:51:08.600 --> 00:51:12.333
our plowing and school resource officers in

00:51:13.266 --> 00:51:17.100
fiscal year '26-'27. And

00:51:17.166 --> 00:51:20.300
that was an additional $360,000.

00:51:21.000 --> 00:51:21.166
I

00:51:23.166 --> 00:51:26.333
really appreciate so many school board members showing up tonight, and

00:51:27.400 --> 00:51:31.266
as well as parents who are here, and I think everybody has

00:51:31.367 --> 00:51:32.400
said this

00:51:34.734 --> 00:51:38.600
has been a difficult... It's been the most difficult budget I've

00:51:38.667 --> 00:51:42.433
ever seen in my years in any kind of

00:51:42.433 --> 00:51:44.300
public service. So,

00:51:45.533 --> 00:51:49.300
it's trying to come up with that balance between what we think

00:51:49.333 --> 00:51:53.233
that is reasonable to ask and what we think will result

00:51:53.300 --> 00:51:57.066
in passing of a budget. So that's really what's key for

00:51:57.133 --> 00:51:57.767
us is,

00:51:58.700 --> 00:52:01.967
if you listen to Mr. Cobb or you listen to some other people,

00:52:05.834 --> 00:52:05.934
I

00:52:07.367 --> 00:52:09.500
speak with people who are on fixed incomes.

00:52:10.033 --> 00:52:13.700
They're our taxpayers, too. So what can we

00:52:14.700 --> 00:52:16.433
ask for that we think

00:52:17.834 --> 00:52:21.400
will sustain a yes vote? And

00:52:22.233 --> 00:52:25.700
I think what we have heard

00:52:25.734 --> 00:52:29.433
consistently from our finance

00:52:29.500 --> 00:52:30.433
director is

00:52:31.567 --> 00:52:34.233
that if we continue to put money

00:52:35.533 --> 00:52:38.433
into the budget that we can't sustain the next year.

00:52:38.600 --> 00:52:41.567
So if we go back to just adding in

00:52:41.600 --> 00:52:45.433
positions, as much as people really want to do that,

00:52:46.634 --> 00:52:50.567
that's going to get us, to use Abigail's expression, kicking the can

00:52:50.600 --> 00:52:54.500
down the road. So then we are going to be back

00:52:54.533 --> 00:52:56.734
here again in another year. So,

00:52:58.433 --> 00:52:58.734
I think

00:52:59.800 --> 00:53:03.066
with the request for the snow plowing and the school resource

00:53:03.233 --> 00:53:07.033
officers, it would be up to the council if you approve

00:53:07.100 --> 00:53:10.667
that, and if you approve that for one year or on an ongoing

00:53:10.734 --> 00:53:12.000
basis, we'd be thrilled.

00:53:14.066 --> 00:53:14.634
30 seconds.

00:53:15.000 --> 00:53:15.734
Yes. Thank you.

00:53:17.033 --> 00:53:20.967
And otherwise, looking for what feels

00:53:21.033 --> 00:53:23.300
best to this council and

00:53:24.333 --> 00:53:28.300
hopefully supporting us on a request for another percent that would

00:53:28.333 --> 00:53:32.233
help fund our fund balance. Which we're also doing in some

00:53:32.300 --> 00:53:35.967
savings that we received in our health insurance premium that did

00:53:36.066 --> 00:53:39.233
not result in being as high as we had expected.

00:53:39.333 --> 00:53:42.834
So some of that savings is also going into our contingency.

00:53:43.300 --> 00:53:45.066
So we're working on that-

00:53:45.166 --> 00:53:45.166
Time

00:53:45.166 --> 00:53:46.700
... as much as possible. Thank you.

00:53:47.600 --> 00:53:50.467
Thank you very much. I'm just making a quick note.

00:53:51.734 --> 00:53:53.433
Do we have more public comment?

00:53:55.500 --> 00:53:58.634
All right. We're getting serious about this. All right.

00:53:58.734 --> 00:54:02.667
Well, if there isn't additional public comment on this item, public

00:54:02.700 --> 00:54:06.600
comment is closed. I think we heard some questions for our

00:54:06.734 --> 00:54:10.667
city manager, and I think that the

00:54:10.700 --> 00:54:14.533
city manager also has some information to share that might

00:54:14.834 --> 00:54:16.900
help council's discussion. So

00:54:17.934 --> 00:54:18.800
we're getting into it.

00:54:21.000 --> 00:54:24.934
So to answer some of the questions, I'm happy for others to chime

00:54:25.033 --> 00:54:25.800
in. I know

00:54:27.634 --> 00:54:31.433
Carlton had suggested, always a good idea, what are some other

00:54:31.567 --> 00:54:35.300
creative revenue sources to take a look at, because

00:54:35.533 --> 00:54:39.500
as we all know, most of our budget, both in the school and the

00:54:39.600 --> 00:54:39.900
city

00:54:41.000 --> 00:54:42.800
sides, are reliant on the property tax.

00:54:42.867 --> 00:54:46.800
We've got other funding sources, but that's a big source of the funding for both of

00:54:46.834 --> 00:54:49.767
those budgets. And so, are there ways to look at that?

00:54:50.300 --> 00:54:50.500
The

00:54:51.834 --> 00:54:54.500
problem in Maine is that the

00:54:56.000 --> 00:55:00.066
legislature has preempted municipalities

00:55:00.066 --> 00:55:02.300
from doing anything with property taxation.

00:55:02.333 --> 00:55:06.033
So we hear people say sometimes, "Hey, why don't you tax someone's extra home?"

00:55:06.133 --> 00:55:07.900
Or if they're somewhat using it for

00:55:08.033 --> 00:55:11.634
a-Airbnb or something. We are

00:55:11.734 --> 00:55:15.667
legally prohibited from charging one tax rate to

00:55:15.667 --> 00:55:18.734
a certain group and another to other folks.

00:55:18.834 --> 00:55:22.800
Same way we can't institute our own local sales

00:55:22.800 --> 00:55:23.233
tax,

00:55:24.300 --> 00:55:28.033
especially on the motels, which if you look at the studies, are mostly paid for

00:55:28.033 --> 00:55:31.934
by folks that are not from South Portland, either out of state or

00:55:32.000 --> 00:55:35.500
out of town, and that would help spread some of the burden that we have for cost.

00:55:35.533 --> 00:55:39.066
But we can't even do that without permission from the legislature, and that's

00:55:39.066 --> 00:55:41.734
something this council has been trying to work on

00:55:42.500 --> 00:55:46.333
for many years, but in particular, especially since two years ago when we had the

00:55:46.333 --> 00:55:49.333
big revaluation. And it's something that's gained momentum, but

00:55:50.734 --> 00:55:53.634
similar to the question about assessed values, we also can't

00:55:56.400 --> 00:55:59.600
tell one group, "Hey, we don't like what you're doing, so we're going to assess

00:55:59.667 --> 00:56:02.333
your property more." That would be kind of weaponizing

00:56:03.166 --> 00:56:06.700
the assessing process, and we're not allowed by statute to do that.

00:56:07.634 --> 00:56:11.567
We have a process that's established effectively by

00:56:11.600 --> 00:56:13.667
the state for how we assess different properties.

00:56:13.700 --> 00:56:17.333
And just remember, if we increase the value of one property, the city doesn't get

00:56:17.333 --> 00:56:20.900
more revenue out of that. What that effectively serves to do is it impacts the mill

00:56:20.967 --> 00:56:24.300
rate, because the total amount we need to raise from

00:56:24.367 --> 00:56:24.967
taxes,

00:56:26.233 --> 00:56:28.233
let's say we need 100 million in taxes.

00:56:28.934 --> 00:56:32.133
If we're raising the assessed value of

00:56:33.000 --> 00:56:36.767
a group of homes or a group of businesses, that doesn't raise us more money.

00:56:37.133 --> 00:56:40.233
What it does is it impacts the formula, and it impacts the mill rate, and it means

00:56:40.266 --> 00:56:44.066
the mill rate's lower than it would otherwise be, but we

00:56:44.100 --> 00:56:45.734
don't raise more money from that.

00:56:47.734 --> 00:56:51.734
I think we'll talk about in a minute this idea of collaborating

00:56:51.734 --> 00:56:55.200
or the good idea about what Portland went through back

00:56:57.000 --> 00:56:58.767
20-ish years ago.

00:57:00.433 --> 00:57:04.200
I'm trying to think here. Oh, there was a question about the law and the school

00:57:04.266 --> 00:57:07.800
budget being passed in June. I actually don't know the answer to that.

00:57:07.867 --> 00:57:11.467
Do any of the school officials know whether there's a state law?

00:57:11.600 --> 00:57:14.600
Jessica, of course. Our city clerk, Jessica Hughes.

00:57:16.133 --> 00:57:16.867
Lucky she's here.

00:57:17.800 --> 00:57:18.967
I just looked up the statute.

00:57:19.333 --> 00:57:19.934
Sorry.

00:57:22.033 --> 00:57:26.033
Hi, Jessica Hughes, city clerk. So school budget validation referendum has

00:57:26.066 --> 00:57:29.934
to pass before July. So there is a time deadline that we do

00:57:29.967 --> 00:57:33.734
have to do that. If it does not pass by the deadline that is in statute,

00:57:34.233 --> 00:57:37.867
the FY26 budget would carry through for operational until a

00:57:37.967 --> 00:57:41.800
budget is passed. So that's why our budget is in June, because we are having to

00:57:41.834 --> 00:57:42.867
have it approved by July.

00:57:44.166 --> 00:57:45.233
Great. Thanks, Jessica.

00:57:45.800 --> 00:57:48.767
Thank you.

00:57:49.200 --> 00:57:53.033
I'm going to share my screen because I had anticipated a few of

00:57:53.100 --> 00:57:56.734
these questions here, and I think it's sometimes helpful if people can see them

00:57:56.800 --> 00:58:00.600
visually what I'm talking about. So I understood the

00:58:00.967 --> 00:58:04.834
idea of collaboration and cost savings would come up, and we'd be very

00:58:04.900 --> 00:58:08.467
happy. I don't know if it needs to be because it's a request from the school board

00:58:08.533 --> 00:58:10.400
or if staff simply just want to get together,

00:58:11.400 --> 00:58:14.834
and start talking about whether there are ways to see if we can

00:58:15.800 --> 00:58:18.100
reduce overall cost to taxpayers through

00:58:18.400 --> 00:58:22.200
consolidation of services or cooperative purchasing

00:58:22.233 --> 00:58:25.000
arrangements or any sort of these different things.

00:58:25.233 --> 00:58:29.200
And we're also fortunate, our finance director was literally working as the

00:58:29.200 --> 00:58:32.600
finance director in Portland the time that the MOU that was referenced was in

00:58:32.634 --> 00:58:35.000
place. So we have someone that has experience with

00:58:36.533 --> 00:58:39.100
that, and so, a good thing to build on.

00:58:40.000 --> 00:58:43.100
I think one of the things that is important, and we'll be upfront, is I do think it

00:58:43.133 --> 00:58:47.000
is important that the costs are accurately accounted for

00:58:47.166 --> 00:58:50.634
where they're incurred. And as superintendent knows, we've

00:58:51.400 --> 00:58:54.500
worked for a couple of years. We had a consultant come in and help us.

00:58:54.600 --> 00:58:58.300
We frankly believe this is going to be turning some of the conversation on its

00:58:58.333 --> 00:59:01.967
head. We believe there's a lot of costs the city right now is incurring that are

00:59:02.033 --> 00:59:04.700
more appropriately should be shown in the school budget.

00:59:04.734 --> 00:59:08.600
For example, as the study showed, the city could fire two parks

00:59:08.700 --> 00:59:12.467
workers today because all they do is work on school athletic fields and

00:59:12.500 --> 00:59:15.500
things of that nature, but it shows up in our side of the budget.

00:59:15.533 --> 00:59:18.567
And there's a lot of examples of that, and so,

00:59:19.467 --> 00:59:22.634
I think part of that discussion should be where should these costs lie?

00:59:22.700 --> 00:59:24.900
Those things don't really save taxpayers' dollars, right?

00:59:24.967 --> 00:59:28.533
Because it's either showing up on our side of the budget or the school, but it does

00:59:28.567 --> 00:59:32.533
give a more accurate representation of when we split up that dollar we

00:59:32.600 --> 00:59:36.367
receive, every dollar we receive in taxes, what is it really going

00:59:36.400 --> 00:59:37.900
towards?

00:59:39.133 --> 00:59:42.967
With that, I understand the school folks have had

00:59:43.033 --> 00:59:45.500
a... I'll say I've been doing this work for 20 years.

00:59:45.533 --> 00:59:47.800
This is one of a handful of incidents where I've seen,

00:59:48.734 --> 00:59:50.200
four different communities I've worked in.

00:59:50.233 --> 00:59:53.600
This is probably one of the top three or four toughest discussions, so I

00:59:53.634 --> 00:59:57.467
really feel for you all. I've been on the other side of it in some of the other

00:59:57.600 --> 00:59:58.266
communities.

00:59:59.634 --> 01:00:03.400
But it's not tough when you've got teachers that are looking to be

01:00:03.433 --> 01:00:06.634
laid off and parents that are upset and

01:00:07.500 --> 01:00:09.400
it's not something I know you all envy.

01:00:09.500 --> 01:00:13.166
So, I completely understand why there'd want to be discussion about

01:00:13.200 --> 01:00:15.834
moving costs under the city's share of the budget.

01:00:15.867 --> 01:00:19.700
I guess my message more towards the council would be,

01:00:20.233 --> 01:00:24.233
there's two things that would happen if those costs for the SRO and for

01:00:24.300 --> 01:00:26.300
snow plowing are absorbed on our side of the budget.

01:00:26.333 --> 01:00:28.100
We either need to increase taxes,

01:00:28.967 --> 01:00:31.500
again, showing up on our side of the budget to fund that

01:00:31.500 --> 01:00:34.600
$360,000, or if

01:00:35.667 --> 01:00:39.567
we can't increase that taxes any more than we have, we need to now go

01:00:39.600 --> 01:00:43.367
find $360,000 in cuts on our side of the

01:00:43.433 --> 01:00:45.467
budget to make up for those shifted costs.

01:00:46.233 --> 01:00:49.734
And so if it's the first scenario where we need to increase taxes, again, that

01:00:49.800 --> 01:00:52.033
skews where the costs are actually occurring.

01:00:53.033 --> 01:00:55.500
There's a possibility, depending on what costs are shift.

01:00:55.600 --> 01:00:56.700
I don't know if these ones

01:00:57.567 --> 01:01:01.266
would be eligible, but the school gets EPS funding for certain

01:01:01.333 --> 01:01:05.000
costs that it has, and if they're shifted to the school, there's a question of, are

01:01:05.100 --> 01:01:08.033
those now not reimbursable by the

01:01:08.066 --> 01:01:11.834
state?Most importantly, it doesn't save the taxpayers any money because we're still

01:01:11.900 --> 01:01:15.533
raising taxes. So that begs the question, why not just allow the

01:01:15.634 --> 01:01:19.300
school budget to increase taxes to cover these costs?

01:01:20.166 --> 01:01:23.967
If no tax increase is acceptable and we need to cut in our budget,

01:01:24.600 --> 01:01:28.166
I guess the question is, is it equitable to make the city cut services or

01:01:28.233 --> 01:01:32.166
position to fund school costs? Um, and also bring up the

01:01:32.200 --> 01:01:35.934
example a couple of years ago, when we sent out tax bills that were $4 million

01:01:36.000 --> 01:01:37.000
less than they should have been,

01:01:38.033 --> 01:01:41.233
the proportional, equitable thing to do would've been to say, "All right, school,

01:01:41.634 --> 01:01:45.100
2.6 million of that loss in revenue is yours to find."

01:01:45.767 --> 01:01:49.233
We didn't do that. It was our error, and we ate it by doing

01:01:49.300 --> 01:01:53.166
position freezes, spending freezes, different things of that nature, because

01:01:53.233 --> 01:01:55.000
it was our error. And, um,

01:01:56.200 --> 01:01:57.767
I just think that's worth mentioning.

01:01:58.333 --> 01:02:00.467
I also think it's worth mentioning that the city has been...

01:02:00.500 --> 01:02:03.533
And I know especially because of that Mr.

01:02:03.800 --> 01:02:07.634
Cobbs and others' points about the assessed property values

01:02:07.734 --> 01:02:11.667
going up and that having such an impact on folks, um, and

01:02:11.734 --> 01:02:15.133
so you've seen tax increases. On the city side of the budget since

01:02:15.266 --> 01:02:18.533
FY22, we've added 15.5 positions, but we've

01:02:18.567 --> 01:02:21.900
eliminated 16.25, so we're net loss of

01:02:22.200 --> 01:02:26.100
0.75 positions during that time, and we've

01:02:26.200 --> 01:02:29.700
got another two and a half additional FTEs proposed to be

01:02:29.734 --> 01:02:33.300
eliminated in this budget, so that would bring us down to negative

01:02:33.533 --> 01:02:37.300
3.25 positions, um, since FY22. However, the

01:02:37.333 --> 01:02:40.934
demand for our services, as I talked about during our budget presentation last

01:02:41.000 --> 01:02:42.500
week, whether it's, um,

01:02:43.333 --> 01:02:43.467
uh,

01:02:45.133 --> 01:02:48.033
a request for service through, um, uh, car

01:02:48.100 --> 01:02:52.033
registrations or for, uh, road work and the number

01:02:52.100 --> 01:02:55.800
of storms that we've had, or just IT demands, uh, has

01:02:55.834 --> 01:02:59.634
actually increased. And so we're cutting positions, but we're seeing increases.

01:03:01.433 --> 01:03:04.834
Um, so anyway, I think that's all I wanted to share on that.

01:03:04.900 --> 01:03:08.166
And then just finally, fund balance, I think one thing we haven't really talked

01:03:08.233 --> 01:03:12.166
about here is, um, whether it's one million or two million or three

01:03:12.233 --> 01:03:15.700
million, um, that probably is maybe a conservative number.

01:03:16.066 --> 01:03:17.867
Um, what are we going to do about that?

01:03:17.934 --> 01:03:21.934
Because whether it's a million or two or three,

01:03:22.000 --> 01:03:25.100
that's going to eat into the city's fund balance, and those are dollars that,

01:03:25.834 --> 01:03:29.634
um, next year, we would've used to buy capital items like

01:03:29.667 --> 01:03:32.967
fire trucks and public works trucks and parks

01:03:33.333 --> 01:03:37.233
equipment to maintain our parks. So if we don't have that money and the

01:03:37.300 --> 01:03:40.300
school doesn't repay it, there's two options again.

01:03:40.333 --> 01:03:43.900
We raise taxes to buy those things, and now

01:03:43.934 --> 01:03:47.734
on our side, we have to raise taxes for money that we should have had, so

01:03:47.800 --> 01:03:50.667
it skews where they should be shown.

01:03:51.500 --> 01:03:54.200
Or we just go without those capital equipment.

01:03:54.266 --> 01:03:58.033
And I guess that's a question really for the council is, is that acceptable or does

01:03:58.066 --> 01:04:01.967
there need to be, um, a plan there as well for

01:04:02.300 --> 01:04:05.367
repayment? Because that's ultimately what we'd look to pursue.

01:04:05.700 --> 01:04:08.834
There's no easy answers because where's the school get the money to repay it?

01:04:08.867 --> 01:04:11.900
Because right now they don't even have enough in their own fund balance.

01:04:11.967 --> 01:04:15.767
So there's no good options here, and I understand that, but I

01:04:16.200 --> 01:04:20.033
think it's worth considering before we talk about whether we shift costs or not to

01:04:20.100 --> 01:04:23.133
make sure we're factoring in all those different things.

01:04:23.200 --> 01:04:26.500
So hopefully that answers questions and clarifies a few things.

01:04:26.567 --> 01:04:26.634
Um,

01:04:28.300 --> 01:04:28.533
sorry.

01:04:29.500 --> 01:04:30.400
Take a question.

01:04:31.300 --> 01:04:33.467
Yeah, I had indicated we can't assess...

01:04:33.700 --> 01:04:36.700
Um, sorry, just to reiterate. We can't assess,

01:04:37.533 --> 01:04:39.834
um, based on whether we think someone's a

01:04:40.934 --> 01:04:42.767
bad actor, they're emitting too much.

01:04:42.967 --> 01:04:46.333
You can't do property value assessments on those.

01:04:46.433 --> 01:04:50.400
Um, and in fact, over the years when we've assessed some of the tags,

01:04:50.433 --> 01:04:53.300
some of them have challenged that assessment and been successful in getting it

01:04:53.333 --> 01:04:56.934
reduced. So they are being

01:04:57.000 --> 01:05:00.433
increased this year, I do know that, but, um, we can't just

01:05:00.500 --> 01:05:03.934
arbitrarily, especially this council doesn't control

01:05:03.967 --> 01:05:07.333
assessments. It's done through, um, a process defined by the state.

01:05:07.433 --> 01:05:10.767
And so, um, right now we don't have evidence that

01:05:11.266 --> 01:05:15.133
they're being, um, uh, under-assessed, or at least we can't go in and

01:05:15.200 --> 01:05:18.900
say, "Hey, we're going to jack your assessment up because we want you to pay more

01:05:18.934 --> 01:05:19.834
in taxes." That's not

01:05:20.934 --> 01:05:22.834
how it legally works, um,

01:05:23.667 --> 01:05:24.300
right or wrong.

01:05:26.767 --> 01:05:27.533
Okay. That's it.

01:05:29.166 --> 01:05:32.934
Thank you. Thank you very much, and thank you again to everyone who made public

01:05:33.000 --> 01:05:36.734
comment. Thank you to my fellow councilors for

01:05:36.800 --> 01:05:39.900
what we are about to discuss, which I believe...

01:05:40.767 --> 01:05:42.667
Yeah, can I just say what I

01:05:43.700 --> 01:05:47.200
think what we're being asked to give a reaction to is,

01:05:47.400 --> 01:05:47.700
um,

01:05:49.300 --> 01:05:53.300
any percentage guidance we would give back to

01:05:53.300 --> 01:05:57.233
the school department, whether it's 1% or different than 1%.

01:05:58.200 --> 01:05:59.700
Um, and also

01:06:00.800 --> 01:06:04.700
this guidance I think was going more to the city manager on

01:06:06.066 --> 01:06:09.967
just a reaction to how you would feel about the school budget

01:06:10.066 --> 01:06:13.300
changing by the amount of the SROs

01:06:13.467 --> 01:06:15.834
and snow removal

01:06:16.700 --> 01:06:19.934
if the city absorbed that. Ms. Councilor Matthews.

01:06:20.800 --> 01:06:21.033
Okay.

01:06:22.266 --> 01:06:25.767
I'm going to take a breath because my wife said sometimes I sound very overbearing

01:06:25.800 --> 01:06:28.166
when I'm talking. I think it's over passionate.

01:06:28.900 --> 01:06:29.100
Um,

01:06:30.433 --> 01:06:32.500
so, uh, wow.

01:06:33.367 --> 01:06:34.500
How'd we get here?

01:06:35.767 --> 01:06:39.100
Five years ago, we weren't here. What a long five years.

01:06:40.100 --> 01:06:43.600
Anyway, I got a lot of questions for the, uh, school department.

01:06:43.734 --> 01:06:43.934
Um,

01:06:45.400 --> 01:06:48.400
there's a lot of people... I had

01:06:49.133 --> 01:06:52.934
72 texts today, which was completely ironic that 36 was begging me

01:06:53.000 --> 01:06:56.000
not to agree to raise

01:06:56.900 --> 01:07:00.700
your percentage, and 36 saying, "Please, please, please, save the

01:07:00.767 --> 01:07:03.433
teachers, save positions, everything."

01:07:04.367 --> 01:07:04.533
Well,

01:07:05.734 --> 01:07:06.433
here's the thing,

01:07:07.400 --> 01:07:11.166
is I am 100% pro-school, pro-teacher,

01:07:11.600 --> 01:07:15.100
pro-student, everything. Gave 12 years of my life to the

01:07:15.133 --> 01:07:18.967
schools.And we're asking

01:07:19.300 --> 01:07:21.133
the public to go ahead and

01:07:24.400 --> 01:07:28.300
pay higher taxes, and some of those people we're asking to pay

01:07:28.400 --> 01:07:30.066
higher taxes, we laid off.

01:07:32.033 --> 01:07:33.200
What an abomination.

01:07:34.367 --> 01:07:35.767
I'm

01:07:36.000 --> 01:07:40.567
going

01:07:40.900 --> 01:07:44.634
to ask Abigail to go to the podium, if you would, please, because I got some

01:07:44.700 --> 01:07:48.700
questions. So I've watched the school board meetings, followed it

01:07:48.700 --> 01:07:52.233
pretty closely. I've talked to several school board members, several parents.

01:07:53.800 --> 01:07:56.000
First of all, I don't want to see any schools close.

01:07:56.066 --> 01:07:59.634
I don't like the thought of the reconfiguration now.

01:08:02.400 --> 01:08:05.233
So when the health savings, it came in at 5%.

01:08:06.100 --> 01:08:09.500
All right? What's that give us, about 600,000 savings,

01:08:09.767 --> 01:08:10.533
700? More?

01:08:11.066 --> 01:08:13.667
Yeah. We budgeted at 12%, and it came in at

01:08:13.934 --> 01:08:17.900
5.14%. It yielded about 655 or

01:08:17.934 --> 01:08:18.433
so thousand.

01:08:18.834 --> 01:08:20.900
Okay. Where did that money go into the new budget?

01:08:21.634 --> 01:08:25.500
So in our current budget, 179,000 of that is in

01:08:25.600 --> 01:08:28.133
contingency with the idea towards fund balance.

01:08:28.734 --> 01:08:31.834
The rest is with adding back positions.

01:08:31.867 --> 01:08:35.600
Okay. Now, by adding back those positions that you

01:08:35.634 --> 01:08:36.600
guys felt were needed,

01:08:37.600 --> 01:08:37.934
okay?

01:08:38.834 --> 01:08:41.400
And again, our job is not for the public.

01:08:41.433 --> 01:08:44.700
Our job is not to micromanage the school board and

01:08:45.767 --> 01:08:49.066
what they've cut and what they've decided, and I'm not trying to do that.

01:08:49.166 --> 01:08:50.634
I'm just asking questions.

01:08:53.500 --> 01:08:57.066
The positions that you decided to eliminate, I had

01:08:58.166 --> 01:09:01.500
grandmothers calling me, it was awful, crying that they were losing their

01:09:01.634 --> 01:09:04.367
10-hour job

01:09:05.900 --> 01:09:06.300
in the

01:09:07.233 --> 01:09:08.166
cafeterias.

01:09:10.000 --> 01:09:13.133
I just can't

01:09:13.233 --> 01:09:16.634
fathom what lunch aids

01:09:17.734 --> 01:09:21.200
could possibly do to a $74 million

01:09:21.567 --> 01:09:24.600
budget at $15 an hour or whatever.

01:09:25.367 --> 01:09:28.934
And I've listened

01:09:29.000 --> 01:09:31.900
to Dr. Prince. Is it Dr. Prince? Dr.

01:09:32.133 --> 01:09:33.800
Prince preached that,

01:09:35.400 --> 01:09:36.533
in a respectful way,

01:09:38.467 --> 01:09:41.600
that we don't get rid of anybody at the top

01:09:41.934 --> 01:09:45.800
because it's a domino effect of legality

01:09:45.900 --> 01:09:49.834
and protecting the students. But when we have a lot of

01:09:49.900 --> 01:09:51.400
top-heavy people,

01:09:52.300 --> 01:09:56.133
we could possibly remove some of

01:09:56.166 --> 01:09:58.734
those positions. We really could

01:09:59.634 --> 01:10:03.600
remove some of those positions and add that to the contingency and

01:10:03.700 --> 01:10:04.934
keep it at the 6%.

01:10:07.533 --> 01:10:08.400
We have a lot of

01:10:10.100 --> 01:10:11.533
social workers. We have a lot of

01:10:13.800 --> 01:10:14.500
counselors. We

01:10:16.400 --> 01:10:20.033
just have a lot of top brass that are salaried positions that are not face-to-face

01:10:20.100 --> 01:10:23.600
with children, and this is what this education system is about.

01:10:23.734 --> 01:10:27.433
It's about face-to-face. We try to get

01:10:27.500 --> 01:10:31.333
our children away from as much screen time as possible and put them in front of a

01:10:31.400 --> 01:10:35.400
teacher, and I think it's the teachers that

01:10:35.500 --> 01:10:37.200
make a difference in a child's life.

01:10:39.166 --> 01:10:41.567
I know there's a lot of good administrators, don't get me wrong.

01:10:41.634 --> 01:10:41.934
They make

01:10:42.900 --> 01:10:44.500
differences, too. But

01:10:45.400 --> 01:10:49.033
is there any way that we could really take a look at this budget

01:10:49.500 --> 01:10:50.567
and possibly

01:10:52.266 --> 01:10:54.734
trim the fat in the

01:10:55.700 --> 01:10:59.567
higher salaried positions to add to the contingency of the

01:10:59.734 --> 01:11:03.400
179 and get it back to the 600,000 or the

01:11:03.700 --> 01:11:06.233
700,000? I don't

01:11:07.634 --> 01:11:11.400
understand how we took 70 positions, and they were

01:11:11.433 --> 01:11:15.100
probably some of the lowest positions on the totem pole. All right?

01:11:15.133 --> 01:11:17.867
That's my first question. Second question is, does

01:11:18.233 --> 01:11:20.700
consolidating Kayla's school

01:11:22.233 --> 01:11:26.233
cost any more bus driving wise, bus aide wise?

01:11:26.500 --> 01:11:29.834
Is that going to cause more

01:11:29.900 --> 01:11:32.266
congestion places? Have we looked at that?

01:11:33.767 --> 01:11:37.200
Why don't you answer those first two, and then we'll go from there.

01:11:37.800 --> 01:11:38.133
Okay.

01:11:39.100 --> 01:11:41.200
Yeah. I appreciate these questions very much.

01:11:42.000 --> 01:11:43.166
The first one about the

01:11:44.934 --> 01:11:48.800
lunch aides, if you add them all up together, it's

01:11:48.900 --> 01:11:51.700
around 55 or so thousand plus payroll taxes.

01:11:52.133 --> 01:11:54.000
Add it all up together, that's almost an ed tech.

01:11:55.300 --> 01:11:56.100
That's a decision,

01:11:57.100 --> 01:11:58.467
right? It is a decision,

01:11:59.533 --> 01:12:02.400
but we get to keep an ed tech for that.

01:12:03.300 --> 01:12:06.133
And then we figured, well, we often have vacancies there anyway.

01:12:06.767 --> 01:12:10.433
Obviously, we don't want to put anybody out of a

01:12:10.500 --> 01:12:14.300
beloved 10-hour-a-week job that they really cherish and that the students enjoy as

01:12:14.400 --> 01:12:14.634
well.

01:12:16.667 --> 01:12:17.900
That is the calculus,

01:12:18.800 --> 01:12:19.900
that it saves an ed tech.

01:12:21.834 --> 01:12:25.800
I think the administrative director versus

01:12:25.834 --> 01:12:26.800
student facing,

01:12:28.333 --> 01:12:31.834
there's a lot of different opinions on that one.

01:12:32.700 --> 01:12:35.667
There's a lot of valid opinions on that one.

01:12:36.533 --> 01:12:39.634
I think every sector has its kind of

01:12:39.934 --> 01:12:43.700
frontline and its inner operational engine.

01:12:45.900 --> 01:12:48.133
Think about the Artemis launch

01:12:49.100 --> 01:12:51.500
that we've recently watched. I watched that with my children.

01:12:52.400 --> 01:12:55.634
I remember watching that, and I saw these four

01:12:56.934 --> 01:12:59.033
interplanetary-facing employees

01:13:02.233 --> 01:13:05.900
supported by buildings of administrators,

01:13:06.767 --> 01:13:10.367
engineers, researchers, HR people that help them with all their

01:13:10.467 --> 01:13:11.033
issues.

01:13:12.900 --> 01:13:16.867
So there's something to be said for the operational engine

01:13:16.900 --> 01:13:17.033
that

01:13:18.400 --> 01:13:20.233
enables the teachers to do their best work.

01:13:21.300 --> 01:13:25.200
And I'm not denying there's notGood decisions either

01:13:25.300 --> 01:13:26.767
way on what you're saying.

01:13:28.200 --> 01:13:29.700
It's a tough position to be in.

01:13:31.433 --> 01:13:32.934
It's a tough position to say,

01:13:34.100 --> 01:13:35.100
"I, as a director,

01:13:35.934 --> 01:13:37.266
need to be here right now,"

01:13:39.500 --> 01:13:41.400
and think about what that means.

01:13:42.467 --> 01:13:44.533
And we've had to think about that about ourselves.

01:13:44.867 --> 01:13:47.033
And that doesn't make every decision right,

01:13:47.900 --> 01:13:48.367
but

01:13:49.300 --> 01:13:50.133
we do have to

01:13:51.100 --> 01:13:54.900
accept some trade-offs and understand that there is an inner

01:13:55.000 --> 01:13:56.567
engine that supports

01:13:58.634 --> 01:14:01.800
the student-facing employees. And I believe every single employee is

01:14:01.834 --> 01:14:05.734
important. The custodian, okay, they're not student-facing, but they're

01:14:05.767 --> 01:14:06.333
important.

01:14:08.433 --> 01:14:11.400
A teacher's important. I think I'm important.

01:14:13.033 --> 01:14:16.700
I think every single person here is important, and there's no easy choices.

01:14:16.834 --> 01:14:20.700
And I understand the criticism. I think we can take that on the chin.

01:14:23.800 --> 01:14:27.200
Thank you for your question. I don't think I can give you all of the answer you

01:14:27.266 --> 01:14:27.300
need.

01:14:27.400 --> 01:14:28.667
That's fine. Now, as far as the busing?

01:14:29.467 --> 01:14:30.333
As far as the busing,

01:14:31.834 --> 01:14:35.800
we believe that should be similar, primarily because we're paying for drivers

01:14:35.834 --> 01:14:38.033
for all of that time anyway.

01:14:39.266 --> 01:14:41.000
So they remain on the road,

01:14:42.300 --> 01:14:43.734
whether we have the

01:14:44.600 --> 01:14:45.800
Taylor school or not.

01:14:47.467 --> 01:14:48.333
Okay. So

01:14:49.300 --> 01:14:49.934
as far as

01:14:51.100 --> 01:14:54.834
the 179, that's going to go into the general fund?

01:14:55.533 --> 01:14:59.367
Yeah. That goes to contingency. The idea and hope is that we don't

01:14:59.500 --> 01:15:02.433
spend it, the expectation is that we don't spend it, and then it'll eventually go

01:15:02.500 --> 01:15:03.700
into a fund balance.

01:15:04.967 --> 01:15:05.200
Okay.

01:15:05.300 --> 01:15:07.166
And I just want to add, I don't want to

01:15:08.867 --> 01:15:12.667
offend anybody who believes in teachers. My mother was a teacher. I love teachers.

01:15:13.133 --> 01:15:15.166
Right. Oh, and I didn't take it that way at all.

01:15:15.166 --> 01:15:15.800
It is a hard question.

01:15:16.300 --> 01:15:20.200
Do you guys have a plan... Well, first of all, is there any idea what

01:15:20.233 --> 01:15:22.734
the shortfall that the city's going to cover that

01:15:24.133 --> 01:15:27.500
city manager was talking about, it's possibly up to $3 million.

01:15:27.533 --> 01:15:27.734
Mm-hmm.

01:15:27.800 --> 01:15:31.533
And do you guys have a plan to pay back that $3 million?

01:15:31.900 --> 01:15:32.133
Mm-hmm.

01:15:32.200 --> 01:15:32.400
So

01:15:33.100 --> 01:15:36.734
what's

01:15:37.634 --> 01:15:41.266
going to happen is a lot of the taxpayers don't understand is that if you guys

01:15:41.300 --> 01:15:44.834
don't pay that back by next June

01:15:46.700 --> 01:15:47.200
or so-

01:15:47.367 --> 01:15:47.667
Yeah

01:15:48.200 --> 01:15:48.900
... yeah, fiscal year

01:15:49.000 --> 01:15:52.000
... we are already going to have to increase taxes already.

01:15:52.734 --> 01:15:55.834
So is there a plan to pay back that $3

01:15:55.900 --> 01:15:57.900
million in place yet?

01:15:58.000 --> 01:16:01.166
Mm-hmm. Yeah, we're hoping for two at the most.

01:16:01.900 --> 01:16:03.767
But again, there are unknowns, and we'll see.

01:16:05.667 --> 01:16:08.433
I think what I've understood with my conversations with

01:16:09.266 --> 01:16:12.834
your finance director is that we may end up needing to come up with a

01:16:13.000 --> 01:16:16.634
memorandum of understanding where we may make payments on an annual

01:16:16.700 --> 01:16:20.467
basis until we knock that out. I think that could

01:16:20.500 --> 01:16:24.333
be a path, but in the meantime, we do need to hold some

01:16:24.400 --> 01:16:25.033
savings

01:16:26.767 --> 01:16:28.533
in school control for

01:16:29.500 --> 01:16:31.800
a sudden mold issue or something like that.

01:16:32.000 --> 01:16:35.333
And that savings helps support personnel

01:16:35.900 --> 01:16:36.433
long-term.

01:16:36.934 --> 01:16:38.033
Okay. I'm good for right now.

01:16:38.333 --> 01:16:38.333
Thank you.

01:16:38.333 --> 01:16:39.033
Mr. Morelle,

01:16:42.667 --> 01:16:44.934
if they do go to a payment plan, let's say-

01:16:45.200 --> 01:16:45.333
Mm-hmm

01:16:46.333 --> 01:16:50.033
... we still have to keep the tax rate

01:16:50.133 --> 01:16:52.934
higher because we have an outstanding loan, correct?

01:16:54.000 --> 01:16:56.400
It depends. If

01:16:57.200 --> 01:17:00.300
there are certain capital items that we want to purchase that we now don't have the

01:17:00.333 --> 01:17:04.000
fund... Because we use excess fund balance, as you know, to make many CIP

01:17:04.000 --> 01:17:07.900
purchases, capital purchases. And so if that amount is not sufficient

01:17:07.934 --> 01:17:11.834
to fund all the needs that we have, we either don't purchase those

01:17:11.900 --> 01:17:15.266
or we raise taxes in order to purchase those. So that's kind of the two options.

01:17:15.767 --> 01:17:19.634
And yeah, it would be unrealistic to expect that's all repaid in

01:17:19.700 --> 01:17:22.700
one year, frankly. It's a big amount given the situation.

01:17:22.767 --> 01:17:26.066
So it would be over probably a multi-year period, which

01:17:27.533 --> 01:17:30.200
still may have that same impact. We can't fund certain things.

01:17:30.266 --> 01:17:30.567
But it

01:17:31.600 --> 01:17:35.467
also does have some money coming back where it's not quite as dire

01:17:35.500 --> 01:17:36.100
situation.

01:17:36.800 --> 01:17:37.667
All right. Thanks. I'm good.

01:17:38.033 --> 01:17:40.100
Thank you. Thank you, Councilor West.

01:17:40.700 --> 01:17:44.500
Can we put the slide up that shows the budget, the original budget, the

01:17:44.533 --> 01:17:46.166
budget they come before us with,

01:17:46.967 --> 01:17:49.634
and the whatever the other, I forget the third column.

01:17:51.533 --> 01:17:54.634
Is that on a website somewhere I can...

01:17:55.767 --> 01:17:55.900
No.

01:17:59.066 --> 01:17:59.734
Councilor West-

01:17:59.767 --> 01:18:00.433
Sorry.

01:18:00.467 --> 01:18:03.233
... do you have anything else you want to say while they're setting up or not so

01:18:03.266 --> 01:18:03.266
much?

01:18:03.300 --> 01:18:04.400
Well, I think it's-

01:18:04.433 --> 01:18:04.600
Okay

01:18:05.000 --> 01:18:08.800
... really important to get in public exactly what we're

01:18:08.834 --> 01:18:10.033
talking about with this

01:18:12.300 --> 01:18:15.233
vague description of the $2 million or $3 million

01:18:15.300 --> 01:18:16.900
deficit.

01:18:17.900 --> 01:18:21.634
And so I want to start by asking

01:18:21.634 --> 01:18:25.500
that question, because I think the school board came before

01:18:25.600 --> 01:18:28.133
us having said last night that they...

01:18:28.767 --> 01:18:31.033
Oh, and by the way, I just want to tell you, I got to start.

01:18:31.066 --> 01:18:32.900
This I can do without seeing the slide.

01:18:33.634 --> 01:18:37.433
Thank you all, the school board, the members of the community, the staff,

01:18:37.600 --> 01:18:41.066
clearly tremendously difficult year, and people have been working

01:18:41.700 --> 01:18:44.233
very hard to do our best for the schools.

01:18:45.533 --> 01:18:49.200
One bit of trivia when I was looking up if the elections have to take place in

01:18:49.233 --> 01:18:52.033
June, which is state law requires the school elections in June.

01:18:53.000 --> 01:18:56.900
Maine is one of only three states in the nation that requires a vote

01:18:57.033 --> 01:18:58.600
of the people on the school budget.

01:18:59.133 --> 01:19:01.934
And it would seem to me that's something to take a look at in the long term.

01:19:02.000 --> 01:19:03.400
But can we just quickly...

01:19:04.834 --> 01:19:05.033
So

01:19:06.800 --> 01:19:10.300
this board voted last night to come in and ask us for

01:19:10.634 --> 01:19:13.800
7% when we had given guidance at 6%.

01:19:14.233 --> 01:19:18.166
I have no trouble with that. I think we should just say yes, go to the voters with

01:19:18.467 --> 01:19:22.433
7%. Second, the board asked us to consider funding

01:19:22.900 --> 01:19:26.634
snow plowing and the school resource officers for a

01:19:26.700 --> 01:19:30.600
total of about 360,000. I have no

01:19:30.634 --> 01:19:34.133
problem with doing that this year, funding it out of the city

01:19:34.500 --> 01:19:38.333
budgetBut I am concerned about

01:19:38.400 --> 01:19:42.266
what's going on, that we're still, even if we do that,

01:19:42.333 --> 01:19:46.233
there's going to be some kind of a $2 million or $3 million deficit, and I

01:19:46.266 --> 01:19:49.767
would like to hear a better, more complete description

01:19:49.834 --> 01:19:51.033
of that.

01:19:53.734 --> 01:19:54.300
You've got

01:19:55.934 --> 01:19:57.567
on that side at least, the-

01:19:58.033 --> 01:19:58.834
It's on that one.

01:19:59.400 --> 01:20:00.033
So where

01:20:01.367 --> 01:20:03.200
does it show in the budget

01:20:03.834 --> 01:20:07.533
where that deficit is coming from?

01:20:07.600 --> 01:20:10.900
Why are we going to have a $2 or $3 million deficit next year?

01:20:11.867 --> 01:20:13.433
Oh, why are we going to have a-

01:20:13.934 --> 01:20:13.934
Yes.

01:20:14.834 --> 01:20:18.066
Many reasons. I think primarily we're fundamentally

01:20:18.100 --> 01:20:19.900
under-budgeted this fiscal year.

01:20:21.266 --> 01:20:23.967
I don't know that we could've helped that at this point.

01:20:24.967 --> 01:20:26.667
So I think that is a big piece of it.

01:20:27.166 --> 01:20:28.967
I'm sorry, say that again.

01:20:29.600 --> 01:20:29.900
I think

01:20:31.200 --> 01:20:34.967
the primary reason is that we're fundamentally under-budgeted this

01:20:35.000 --> 01:20:37.433
fiscal year. That's my take on it.

01:20:37.634 --> 01:20:40.266
Is the deficit being incurred this fiscal year?

01:20:40.333 --> 01:20:40.800
That's what I'd-

01:20:40.800 --> 01:20:42.634
Yes... like to make clear for the public.

01:20:43.233 --> 01:20:43.233
Yeah.

01:20:43.266 --> 01:20:47.233
So you are going to end this fiscal year $2 million in the red.

01:20:47.266 --> 01:20:47.266
Yeah.

01:20:47.266 --> 01:20:49.433
Is that clear? Is that what the story is?

01:20:49.967 --> 01:20:53.934
Yes. But I'm including the nutrition deficit from

01:20:53.967 --> 01:20:57.634
the prior fiscal year and what I'm projecting to be their deficit this

01:20:57.667 --> 01:20:59.533
fiscal year, and then also our school

01:21:00.433 --> 01:21:01.934
natural deficit this fiscal year.

01:21:02.066 --> 01:21:03.567
So add it all up, I'm

01:21:04.467 --> 01:21:06.467
leaning towards $2 million at the most.

01:21:07.433 --> 01:21:10.900
I agree with $3 million as a nice conservative figure, just in case.

01:21:11.767 --> 01:21:13.066
I understand that.

01:21:13.133 --> 01:21:13.200
Yes.

01:21:13.800 --> 01:21:15.233
I don't think we'll really be there.

01:21:15.467 --> 01:21:19.266
Okay, so just make sure that people in the community understand this, because

01:21:19.367 --> 01:21:23.133
I'm hearing different things. This fiscal year for our schools,

01:21:23.433 --> 01:21:27.367
they are operating in the red to the tune of at least 2 and maybe

01:21:27.433 --> 01:21:28.834
as much as $3 million.

01:21:29.867 --> 01:21:30.066
Right?

01:21:30.333 --> 01:21:30.500
Mm-hmm.

01:21:30.934 --> 01:21:34.300
Right. So then the city is

01:21:34.600 --> 01:21:36.367
prepared to make up that deficit.

01:21:37.734 --> 01:21:37.767
Mm-hmm.

01:21:37.767 --> 01:21:40.767
My question to throw to my colleagues is,

01:21:41.867 --> 01:21:45.834
they've come in for 7%. Why don't we go to

01:21:45.867 --> 01:21:49.767
8% so that we can reduce the amount of

01:21:49.800 --> 01:21:50.600
the deficit

01:21:52.233 --> 01:21:56.066
in a year? Ordinarily, I would never

01:21:56.133 --> 01:21:59.700
do this, but it's because I don't think that it's our job to

01:21:59.767 --> 01:22:01.533
micromanage the school board's budget.

01:22:02.533 --> 01:22:05.734
But we're put in a tremendously difficult situation

01:22:06.967 --> 01:22:10.734
because of things that we didn't do, and really, this actual

01:22:10.834 --> 01:22:12.900
staff that's working on it now, they didn't do.

01:22:13.967 --> 01:22:17.300
But why don't we say we're going to go

01:22:18.166 --> 01:22:19.000
with 8%?

01:22:20.233 --> 01:22:23.634
If 7% is going to give them 540,000,

01:22:23.734 --> 01:22:26.433
then 8% would give them another

01:22:26.634 --> 01:22:30.533
540,000, and that's a

01:22:30.600 --> 01:22:34.567
more fair thing to do than us giving them some kind of a loan and then

01:22:34.634 --> 01:22:36.400
putting them on a repayment schedule.

01:22:39.233 --> 01:22:41.433
So I throw that out to my other counselors for discussion.

01:22:43.000 --> 01:22:44.834
I think that's really the most key thing.

01:22:44.867 --> 01:22:47.667
And as far as the SRO and the snow plowing,

01:22:48.767 --> 01:22:52.467
see, if we were coming in with a zero budget, a balanced

01:22:52.533 --> 01:22:55.467
budget, I'd say, "Yeah, let's give them that for one year.

01:22:55.634 --> 01:22:57.800
Just for one year, we'll pay those costs.

01:22:57.800 --> 01:22:59.533
And then after this, because

01:23:00.533 --> 01:23:04.000
they've gone through a really difficult time, and et cetera.

01:23:04.033 --> 01:23:06.333
And then after this, they can have it be part of their budget."

01:23:07.166 --> 01:23:10.533
But this year is such a big mess, I would just say

01:23:11.100 --> 01:23:14.266
let's just try to go to the voters with eight and

01:23:15.333 --> 01:23:17.333
just explain to the voters what the problem is.

01:23:19.033 --> 01:23:21.467
That's where I'm finished. Thank you for listening.

01:23:22.867 --> 01:23:25.300
Councilor Pride. Thank you very much, Councilor West.

01:23:26.233 --> 01:23:26.467
Yeah.

01:23:27.567 --> 01:23:31.300
And I'm amenable to the 2%. I think I sort of

01:23:31.333 --> 01:23:33.667
indicated that with my question earlier,

01:23:35.233 --> 01:23:36.767
that I was willing to go that extra percent.

01:23:37.634 --> 01:23:41.433
I'm not sure about the plowing and the SROs, and I know that cuts

01:23:41.533 --> 01:23:44.266
into the extra percent because you're going to have to find a way to pay for it.

01:23:44.333 --> 01:23:45.700
But I think

01:23:47.066 --> 01:23:50.166
one thing, and this is what I think we're talking about when it comes to

01:23:50.200 --> 01:23:53.734
transparency, is that if the

01:23:53.767 --> 01:23:57.600
school has a lot to pay for, they have to put that

01:23:57.667 --> 01:24:01.433
all in front of the voter, right? And if things shift and the city

01:24:01.467 --> 01:24:05.233
starts taking care of school responsibilities, then the voter sees that the

01:24:05.367 --> 01:24:07.467
school isn't asking for as much of an increase.

01:24:07.567 --> 01:24:10.967
But the city is asking for more of an increase because we're covering more of those

01:24:11.033 --> 01:24:14.834
costs. And I don't mean this in

01:24:14.967 --> 01:24:17.834
a glib way, but my job gets on the line.

01:24:18.166 --> 01:24:21.667
Because at re-election, the people will see, "Oh, gosh, Councilor

01:24:21.767 --> 01:24:25.734
Pride and the City Council just voted for all this extra spending,"

01:24:26.400 --> 01:24:28.867
when all that extra spending was really for the school.

01:24:29.533 --> 01:24:32.700
It's not our budget that we increased.

01:24:32.800 --> 01:24:35.700
So I think, just in a really basic

01:24:36.166 --> 01:24:38.100
democracy-driven

01:24:39.367 --> 01:24:43.233
thought process here, if people are going to support the school

01:24:43.333 --> 01:24:46.634
budget, they need to know, this is what the school budget is.

01:24:46.734 --> 01:24:48.233
This is what it costs to run our schools.

01:24:48.433 --> 01:24:52.367
We shouldn't be watering that down by having the school take on a

01:24:52.400 --> 01:24:56.333
portion that now the people don't vote on directly.

01:24:56.934 --> 01:24:59.233
They vote for city councilors instead.

01:24:59.867 --> 01:25:02.934
And are they going to vote for those city councilors if they see those city

01:25:03.000 --> 01:25:06.734
councilors continually adding onto their budget to take care of school

01:25:06.800 --> 01:25:09.233
needs? Well, they don't necessarily see that part of it.

01:25:10.066 --> 01:25:12.467
Someone who's really paying attention might see that part of it.

01:25:13.100 --> 01:25:13.300
But

01:25:14.567 --> 01:25:18.333
that's the way that they're going to potentially take that problem,

01:25:19.166 --> 01:25:22.433
is to decide not to elect any one of us.

01:25:24.266 --> 01:25:27.533
And I say that being term limited. I'm not running for re-election.

01:25:27.667 --> 01:25:27.767
But

01:25:31.467 --> 01:25:33.934
what I'm getting at is I think it's really

01:25:35.400 --> 01:25:39.266
part and parcel to the democratic process is having the

01:25:39.333 --> 01:25:41.400
school budget be the honest school budget.

01:25:42.200 --> 01:25:46.100
What are we paying for?And I'm willing to pay the extra

01:25:46.100 --> 01:25:49.667
2%. My value has also gone way up, Ed,

01:25:50.333 --> 01:25:54.066
as everybody's has, because I believe in schools. I really do.

01:25:54.767 --> 01:25:58.533
And in some ways, I know I recommended what, three to five, I think, when we

01:25:58.600 --> 01:26:02.200
first started, but I also asked some questions that I was hoping I'd get a

01:26:02.266 --> 01:26:06.200
presentation that would convince me to go higher about the fact that

01:26:06.266 --> 01:26:09.433
special ed has gone up. We see this

01:26:10.467 --> 01:26:14.233
graph, if you have a graph in your head about enrollment, you see enrollment

01:26:14.266 --> 01:26:18.100
going down, but at the same time, special needs enrollment going way

01:26:18.200 --> 01:26:21.600
up. And the cost of educating a special needs

01:26:21.734 --> 01:26:25.467
student is way higher than educating

01:26:25.600 --> 01:26:27.967
a normal student. And we are mandated.

01:26:28.000 --> 01:26:28.934
And it's funny because

01:26:31.000 --> 01:26:34.700
we recently went to a conference in Washington, and we talked to legislators, and

01:26:35.200 --> 01:26:35.900
we were talking about

01:26:36.800 --> 01:26:39.867
what do we get for mandates from

01:26:40.600 --> 01:26:41.600
the

01:26:42.467 --> 01:26:46.133
federal legislators that aren't funded, and one of them is schools.

01:26:46.500 --> 01:26:50.133
They mandate a certain amount of special education,

01:26:50.767 --> 01:26:53.300
but they only funded that mandate 13%.

01:26:53.834 --> 01:26:53.967
Yep.

01:26:54.867 --> 01:26:55.767
13%.

01:26:56.700 --> 01:27:00.367
If they fully funded that, we wouldn't be in as much of a

01:27:00.433 --> 01:27:00.934
crisis.

01:27:02.266 --> 01:27:05.467
And so, the state is doing sort of the same thing.

01:27:05.500 --> 01:27:06.367
They

01:27:07.467 --> 01:27:11.400
were going to rebalance the funding for schools to give

01:27:11.467 --> 01:27:15.066
more money to our schools, and then they saw that brought their budget too high,

01:27:15.734 --> 01:27:18.767
so they couldn't stomach that, and they reduced it.

01:27:20.367 --> 01:27:24.367
And it just keeps getting passed down to the cities who

01:27:24.367 --> 01:27:28.233
don't have a choice about what kind of tax then they can do, can't target people,

01:27:28.333 --> 01:27:31.600
can't target businesses with taxes because we're not allowed to have a separate

01:27:31.667 --> 01:27:34.934
rate for businesses that we do for residents.

01:27:34.967 --> 01:27:38.900
That's constitutional. Maine constitution dictates, according to the Supreme

01:27:39.000 --> 01:27:42.834
Court, that everybody has to have the same mill rate, which isn't true in

01:27:42.867 --> 01:27:46.500
Massachusetts, by the way. In Massachusetts, commercial mill rate is

01:27:46.567 --> 01:27:47.467
different than

01:27:48.900 --> 01:27:52.000
residential mill rate. So write your legislators.

01:27:52.934 --> 01:27:56.567
Start collecting signatures now to send to the state and to

01:27:56.867 --> 01:27:59.367
amend our constitution so we can do it differently.

01:27:59.767 --> 01:28:02.800
Because if we could have a separate commercial mill rate, we could take a lot of

01:28:02.867 --> 01:28:06.700
these costs off the residents. Because what's happened is

01:28:06.800 --> 01:28:10.066
COVID. COVID made the value of

01:28:10.100 --> 01:28:14.066
commercial buildings go down.

01:28:14.400 --> 01:28:17.600
No one's buying office space anymore. Everybody's working from home.

01:28:18.266 --> 01:28:18.500
So

01:28:19.367 --> 01:28:22.634
those sales of commercial

01:28:23.200 --> 01:28:25.567
real estate are what dictate the assessment,

01:28:26.433 --> 01:28:28.967
right? And so people are paying less for commercial real estate

01:28:30.200 --> 01:28:33.867
across the board, so those values go

01:28:34.000 --> 01:28:34.200
down.

01:28:35.367 --> 01:28:36.133
But I digress.

01:28:38.100 --> 01:28:38.634
Noticed.

01:28:40.767 --> 01:28:43.166
I know. I could feel you staring at me.

01:28:44.233 --> 01:28:45.834
Hoping that I stop talking soon.

01:28:47.800 --> 01:28:49.634
No, it's interesting. It is very interesting.

01:28:49.667 --> 01:28:53.500
But when commercial values go down, that puts more

01:28:53.567 --> 01:28:57.200
taxes on the residents, and that's why we're seeing our residential

01:28:57.266 --> 01:29:01.200
values go up, because we got so many more residential sales than we did commercial

01:29:01.300 --> 01:29:04.200
sales. And when there's more residential sales, it means there's more demand.

01:29:04.233 --> 01:29:06.200
And when there's more demand, the values go up.

01:29:06.767 --> 01:29:08.133
So you all are paying more,

01:29:09.066 --> 01:29:12.700
but we need to be honest about what we're funding with our dollars.

01:29:12.767 --> 01:29:13.867
And so if it's going to

01:29:14.767 --> 01:29:17.266
the schools, it needs to be part of the school budget to go before the

01:29:17.367 --> 01:29:20.166
taxpayer. And I

01:29:21.500 --> 01:29:23.967
really do applaud Councilor Matthews' questions.

01:29:24.000 --> 01:29:24.300
I think

01:29:25.166 --> 01:29:28.967
he, as every parent or everybody in this community, should be

01:29:29.066 --> 01:29:32.900
curious about what the process is with the school making those

01:29:32.967 --> 01:29:36.567
decisions. But in the end, if this council says, "Look,

01:29:37.000 --> 01:29:38.700
we'll go ahead and pass

01:29:39.800 --> 01:29:43.700
a budget that is 8% increase and allow that to go to the

01:29:43.734 --> 01:29:44.367
voters,"

01:29:45.200 --> 01:29:48.266
we don't get to decide what that 2% is. That's the school board.

01:29:48.467 --> 01:29:51.967
So keep attending those meetings. Keep talking to them.

01:29:52.033 --> 01:29:53.100
And honestly,

01:29:54.266 --> 01:29:55.934
they're listening. They really are.

01:29:57.500 --> 01:30:00.300
We don't always seem like we're listening, but we always are.

01:30:01.500 --> 01:30:04.066
I can't tell you how many times I second-guess decisions I made up here

01:30:05.367 --> 01:30:08.000
based on feedback I'm getting from the public.

01:30:08.500 --> 01:30:09.300
So they're listening,

01:30:11.000 --> 01:30:14.967
and I hope that that process continues in a

01:30:14.967 --> 01:30:15.500
way that

01:30:16.500 --> 01:30:18.266
we can all support our schools

01:30:19.133 --> 01:30:21.700
through the ballot initiative. So that's my input.

01:30:22.266 --> 01:30:24.367
Thank you. Thank you very much.

01:30:25.967 --> 01:30:26.367
Others?

01:30:27.533 --> 01:30:29.800
And again, we are ultimately looking to

01:30:30.634 --> 01:30:34.200
give guidance and a response to the snow

01:30:34.233 --> 01:30:36.066
removal and SRO request.

01:30:37.667 --> 01:30:41.400
I realize that this is not our final decision, so I'm going to

01:30:41.800 --> 01:30:45.567
