02:20:36.433 --> 02:20:36.934
There we go.

02:20:37.533 --> 02:20:38.266
And words.

02:20:40.100 --> 02:20:44.033
We're moving on. So I think that you are

02:20:44.033 --> 02:20:48.033
going to lead us, Mr. City Manager, and I think the first department we're talking

02:20:48.100 --> 02:20:50.734
about is the City Clerk's department.

02:20:50.800 --> 02:20:54.233
Yep, and before we get to those, just an overview, especially for newer councilors,

02:20:54.300 --> 02:20:57.433
but you only do this once a year, so just as a refresher.

02:20:57.467 --> 02:20:58.967
So we've split up the departments.

02:20:59.000 --> 02:21:01.567
Some will be going tonight, some will be going in two weeks.

02:21:01.934 --> 02:21:05.767
Each small department has about five to 10 minutes to do an overview.

02:21:06.133 --> 02:21:09.934
Larger get about 10 to 15 to provide you with a brief overview of their budget.

02:21:11.133 --> 02:21:15.000
If councilors wish to add, delete, increase, or decrease

02:21:15.100 --> 02:21:17.300
funding for any sort of budget item,

02:21:18.333 --> 02:21:21.000
we're going to keep track of these and add them to the parking lot.

02:21:22.033 --> 02:21:25.200
So they're not decided on tonight like, "Yes, we're going to fund this new thing,"

02:21:25.233 --> 02:21:27.967
or, "No, we're going to cut this." They're just added to the parking lot, and that

02:21:28.000 --> 02:21:30.667
parking lot workshop is held on May 12th.

02:21:30.700 --> 02:21:33.433
So everything from tonight and everything from two weeks from now that you put in

02:21:33.433 --> 02:21:37.433
the parking lot, that's when you'll determine whether or not those items are

02:21:37.467 --> 02:21:41.333
cut or included or increased or decreased from your final budget

02:21:41.400 --> 02:21:42.800
that you'll vote on in June.

02:21:44.200 --> 02:21:47.667
If you are looking to increase the budget, if there's an item that

02:21:48.166 --> 02:21:51.567
didn't get funded or hadn't even been contemplated and you want to add it, the

02:21:51.567 --> 02:21:55.533
charter does say that if you do make an increase after

02:21:55.567 --> 02:21:58.800
the budget's been presented, if you do make an increase, the total proposed

02:21:58.867 --> 02:22:02.500
expenditures, you also need to increase the total anticipated

02:22:02.567 --> 02:22:06.400
revenue to at least that amount. You can also,

02:22:06.433 --> 02:22:08.600
by inference, cut something else.

02:22:09.100 --> 02:22:12.400
And so what we ask for is when we go through this

02:22:12.433 --> 02:22:16.367
process, something to the effect of, "I would like to

02:22:16.467 --> 02:22:20.300
add, remove, delete X amount in funding for this budget item in

02:22:20.333 --> 02:22:22.767
the clerk department budget."

02:22:24.066 --> 02:22:27.400
And that way, we know specifically what you're asking for and where the funding's

02:22:27.433 --> 02:22:30.066
going to come from, in particular, if you're adding funding.

02:22:30.800 --> 02:22:34.300
Sometimes we've seen councils just add like, "Hey, I don't know if I want to

02:22:37.000 --> 02:22:40.900
fund this or not. I've just got questions." So sometimes there's questions

02:22:40.934 --> 02:22:44.634
that get put in the parking lot like, "I'd like more information about this

02:22:44.667 --> 02:22:46.266
topic, so that's why I'm going to put it there.

02:22:46.333 --> 02:22:49.867
It's not that I want to necessarily cut it." So I guess that's an option as well.

02:22:51.233 --> 02:22:54.867
So with all that said, we're starting off with the city clerk's budget.

02:22:55.300 --> 02:22:58.567
The clerk's budget is up $25,000. That's a

02:22:58.800 --> 02:23:00.300
6.9% increase.

02:23:02.033 --> 02:23:05.533
Additional election costs and replacement voting booths are driving

02:23:06.300 --> 02:23:10.166
much of the increase here. The impact to the median residential

02:23:10.266 --> 02:23:13.767
taxpayer is $9.96 to fund this

02:23:13.834 --> 02:23:17.467
department for the year, and they have no capital budget requests.

02:23:18.100 --> 02:23:21.667
And I will turn it over to our city clerk, Jessica Hughes, to provide

02:23:21.700 --> 02:23:23.767
additional information and answer questions.

02:23:24.166 --> 02:23:24.300
Great.

02:23:24.433 --> 02:23:24.734
Thank you.

02:23:25.567 --> 02:23:29.066
So our additional cost comes predominantly through elections.

02:23:29.634 --> 02:23:33.100
Fees and pricing for that has steadily increased.

02:23:34.033 --> 02:23:37.800
And the additional part of that increase that is not listed

02:23:37.800 --> 02:23:40.834
there is our record management portion.

02:23:40.834 --> 02:23:44.834
We have not preserved or maintained some of our records for over a decade, so I

02:23:44.867 --> 02:23:48.767
am trying to build in a small portion of that

02:23:48.934 --> 02:23:51.567
to get us caught up and then to maintain those records.

02:23:51.700 --> 02:23:54.300
Part of that is the records that we maintain for council,

02:23:55.567 --> 02:23:58.700
but also our vital records that we do produce birth, marriage, and death

02:23:58.734 --> 02:24:02.667
certificates from. So it is not specifically all elections, but majority of that

02:24:02.734 --> 02:24:06.533
cost does come from elections. The piece that we are

02:24:06.567 --> 02:24:10.367
looking to offset some of this is I have budgeted

02:24:10.433 --> 02:24:13.734
for all of our election workers, our wardens, our ward clerks, who administer our

02:24:13.767 --> 02:24:17.333
elections. However, we've given the option to those

02:24:17.367 --> 02:24:21.200
individuals to volunteer their time as a part of their civic duty, and most of

02:24:21.367 --> 02:24:23.734
our election workers want to do that anyway.

02:24:24.166 --> 02:24:27.867
So we did put that in as part of our election clerk application this year that'll

02:24:27.867 --> 02:24:30.667
be coming before council on the 21st,

02:24:31.667 --> 02:24:35.333
and I'll be able to provide a little bit more adjustment to my budget at that

02:24:35.433 --> 02:24:38.367
point. But currently, that's what the increase is for, is to make sure that we are

02:24:38.433 --> 02:24:41.100
paying everyone the minimum wage that is required.

02:24:42.000 --> 02:24:45.500
The replacement of our voting booths is specifically to our community center.

02:24:45.533 --> 02:24:49.266
We have a set, I call them the accordion-style booths.

02:24:49.433 --> 02:24:53.367
Those are our older booths, and every year we have a couple that are damaged, so

02:24:53.400 --> 02:24:55.500
it's reducing the number that we have available.

02:24:55.533 --> 02:24:58.967
By statute, we are required to have so many booths based on our population.

02:25:00.567 --> 02:25:04.367
I am hoping with the savings that I am having in fiscal '26,

02:25:04.767 --> 02:25:06.667
that I might be able to purchase them this year.

02:25:07.767 --> 02:25:11.600
So we do have increases, but again, I'm trying to be very streamlined in how we can

02:25:11.634 --> 02:25:13.467
conserve those funds for our budget.

02:25:15.266 --> 02:25:18.300
The last piece that I will add that came up in conversation during our

02:25:18.367 --> 02:25:22.100
recess is just being mindful of our election process with the school

02:25:22.100 --> 02:25:25.934
budget. It is not built into the budget that we have a school budget that

02:25:26.000 --> 02:25:29.667
is voted down by our voters, and then we have to go back

02:25:29.734 --> 02:25:32.634
to have another school budget validation referendum.

02:25:32.667 --> 02:25:35.700
That is not something that we have seen in our history of South Portland, so it is

02:25:35.767 --> 02:25:39.734
not part of our budget. If we are concerned that that might be a topic

02:25:39.767 --> 02:25:41.400
this year that we want to be mindful of,

02:25:42.634 --> 02:25:46.600
that buffer will allow me to have those funds available to do a secondary

02:25:46.634 --> 02:25:49.834
election. So even though I might be able to purchase the voting booths this fiscal

02:25:49.867 --> 02:25:53.500
year, those funds might be able to be needed if we have to go back for a second

02:25:53.634 --> 02:25:55.033
school budget validation referendum.

02:25:55.934 --> 02:25:59.100
Just as some perspective, because we have not seen that in South Portland, so I do

02:25:59.133 --> 02:26:01.500
want to be transparent with everyone.

02:26:03.600 --> 02:26:04.467
That's all I have for you.

02:26:06.166 --> 02:26:06.767
Thank you.

02:26:10.166 --> 02:26:10.533
Can I ask a question?

02:26:10.567 --> 02:26:12.934
So yeah, we do questions and then public comment and...

02:26:13.533 --> 02:26:15.667
Yes. Sorry, I'm studying your budget.

02:26:16.333 --> 02:26:19.166
Questions from the council. Councilor Walker.

02:26:19.300 --> 02:26:23.000
I had a quick one. And I don't know if this belongs with you or it belongs with a

02:26:23.066 --> 02:26:23.634
different department-

02:26:23.700 --> 02:26:23.700
Sure

02:26:23.700 --> 02:26:27.634
... so feel free to direct me, but who sets parking ticket rates?

02:26:29.000 --> 02:26:31.567
Is that part of the clerk or did you just administer?

02:26:31.667 --> 02:26:34.834
I didn't know with licenses and fees where that landed, so I wanted to ask that

02:26:34.900 --> 02:26:35.166
question.

02:26:35.634 --> 02:26:38.867
Sure. So that actually comes from the department for the fee.

02:26:39.100 --> 02:26:39.333
Okay.

02:26:39.400 --> 02:26:41.266
So we recently had them from the police department.

02:26:41.300 --> 02:26:43.066
That came forward as part of the master fee schedule-

02:26:43.500 --> 02:26:43.500
Okay

02:26:43.533 --> 02:26:45.266
... for two parking fees that were adjusted.

02:26:45.600 --> 02:26:48.367
Okay. So I will save my questions for that for the clerk.

02:26:48.367 --> 02:26:48.567
Please.

02:26:48.667 --> 02:26:48.967
Thank you.

02:26:51.533 --> 02:26:53.567
Other councilors questions for our clerk?

02:26:55.900 --> 02:26:59.634
All right. Do we have public comment on this item, the clerk's office

02:26:59.734 --> 02:27:00.133
budget?

02:27:01.967 --> 02:27:05.800
Anyone? All right. Well, we'll close public

02:27:05.867 --> 02:27:07.200
comment on that topic.

02:27:08.033 --> 02:27:10.467
Counselors, do you want to make any

02:27:11.000 --> 02:27:14.233
recommendations for changes or parking lot items?

02:27:16.500 --> 02:27:17.533
I don't see any.

02:27:18.667 --> 02:27:19.166
Geez.

02:27:20.400 --> 02:27:22.400
Wow, they're all going to be this easy, right?

02:27:23.133 --> 02:27:24.333
You just jinxed us.

02:27:25.033 --> 02:27:28.533
All right. Well, then I guess we move to our next topic.

02:27:28.667 --> 02:27:32.600
Thank you so much, and thank you in advance to any election workers

02:27:32.634 --> 02:27:34.734
who would like to volunteer.

02:27:37.634 --> 02:27:39.500
Next up is our human resources budget.

02:27:39.800 --> 02:27:43.700
This budget is up $6,415. That's an increase of

02:27:43.834 --> 02:27:47.734
1.2%. This budget's annual cost to the median residential

02:27:47.800 --> 02:27:50.233
taxpayer is $40.48.

02:27:51.333 --> 02:27:54.900
Very few highlights here. Really the biggest thing is moving the Civil Service

02:27:55.000 --> 02:27:57.600
Commission budget out of this department,

02:27:58.467 --> 02:28:01.033
as they are now... We've changed the process, as council know.

02:28:01.133 --> 02:28:04.734
You've changed the process for how committees request funding, and those requests

02:28:04.767 --> 02:28:08.600
now show up in the council budget. There are no capital requests here,

02:28:08.700 --> 02:28:12.567
and I'll turn it over to our HR director, Rob Meadow, to talk about any

02:28:12.600 --> 02:28:15.100
other highlights that he wants to.

02:28:15.200 --> 02:28:17.800
Thank you very much, and thank you for giving me a chance to speak tonight.

02:28:18.700 --> 02:28:20.433
Let me just make two very quick points.

02:28:21.667 --> 02:28:25.500
That $20,000 that was typically traditionally part of the

02:28:25.634 --> 02:28:29.500
HR budget for Civil Service Commission, that's now moved over to Civil

02:28:29.533 --> 02:28:33.233
Service Commission. It's no longer part of the HR budget, as the city

02:28:33.300 --> 02:28:36.800
manager is explaining. I did not zero out that line.

02:28:36.900 --> 02:28:40.800
I kept that line at 10,000. The reason I did that is if you look

02:28:40.800 --> 02:28:44.500
at expenditures for civil service-related costs this

02:28:44.634 --> 02:28:47.600
year, there's about 14,000

02:28:48.900 --> 02:28:51.400
this year that was expended on

02:28:52.300 --> 02:28:53.033
promotional

02:28:55.700 --> 02:28:58.634
assessments for police and fire. About 14,000.

02:28:59.767 --> 02:29:03.600
Last year, we spent 17,000 on

02:29:03.934 --> 02:29:07.934
pre-hire psychological evaluations for police and fire.

02:29:08.800 --> 02:29:12.800
If you combine those two costs, we're over $30,000.

02:29:13.433 --> 02:29:17.133
So that's why, to be cautious, I kept some

02:29:17.166 --> 02:29:20.567
money in that line in case we have

02:29:20.600 --> 02:29:24.433
a year where we have significant numbers of new hires in police and

02:29:24.533 --> 02:29:26.700
fire, and we have to do a lot of testing or

02:29:28.333 --> 02:29:32.333
other costs related to that. So if you're wondering why I didn't just zero it

02:29:32.400 --> 02:29:34.333
out, that was my thinking there.

02:29:35.600 --> 02:29:39.367
The only other issue I wanted to mention is, as I mentioned before,

02:29:39.834 --> 02:29:43.433
we're doing really well with the community college refunding

02:29:43.533 --> 02:29:47.467
of training costs for us through the ALFON Grant program.

02:29:47.600 --> 02:29:48.066
Mm-hmm.

02:29:48.100 --> 02:29:48.300
And

02:29:49.700 --> 02:29:53.233
as you know, the ALFON Grant will pay 50%

02:29:53.367 --> 02:29:57.300
of tuition for trainings that our employees

02:29:57.367 --> 02:30:01.233
undertake, provided we comply with their requirements, which

02:30:01.300 --> 02:30:02.400
are quite light.

02:30:04.467 --> 02:30:07.600
Sometimes the payment for these that we get back from the community college is

02:30:07.667 --> 02:30:10.433
rather late. It's hard to predict when we're going to get that.

02:30:10.934 --> 02:30:12.734
But in calendar year '25,

02:30:14.500 --> 02:30:17.200
we brought in a little over $25,000.

02:30:18.533 --> 02:30:22.233
That's money that we would be spending otherwise for this

02:30:22.266 --> 02:30:26.100
training, and we're getting reimbursement for it, thanks in large part to

02:30:27.166 --> 02:30:30.634
my colleague, Lou Cavalero, who handles this program for us.

02:30:30.800 --> 02:30:33.967
So those are the only two items I wanted to mention. Thank you.

02:30:35.400 --> 02:30:38.867
Thank you very much, Rob. All right, questions from

02:30:38.934 --> 02:30:39.800
councilors?

02:30:43.834 --> 02:30:44.600
Councilor Walker?

02:30:44.767 --> 02:30:46.800
I just have a quick question about the office supplies.

02:30:46.900 --> 02:30:50.133
I noticed it was up 160%. Can you talk about...

02:30:51.200 --> 02:30:51.367
We

02:30:52.734 --> 02:30:54.467
had to purchase a photocopier.

02:30:54.600 --> 02:30:54.800
Okay.

02:30:57.233 --> 02:30:57.767
That would do it.

02:30:58.467 --> 02:30:58.600
Yeah.

02:30:59.100 --> 02:30:59.400
Thank you.

02:31:01.734 --> 02:31:03.233
All right. Any other questions?

02:31:04.634 --> 02:31:05.634
What kind of photo...

02:31:06.000 --> 02:31:06.433
Never mind.

02:31:09.100 --> 02:31:09.400
All right.

02:31:10.634 --> 02:31:14.467
Do we have members of the public who'd like to make a comment on this budget,

02:31:15.000 --> 02:31:18.900
the HR budget? Doesn't look like we do, so that

02:31:18.934 --> 02:31:20.500
is closed.

02:31:21.500 --> 02:31:25.066
Counselors, anyone want to recommend any changes or parking lot

02:31:25.200 --> 02:31:25.834
items?

02:31:28.467 --> 02:31:32.400
It doesn't look like there are going to be. No?

02:31:32.800 --> 02:31:33.533
Nope.

02:31:34.500 --> 02:31:35.033
All right.

02:31:36.166 --> 02:31:40.033
I think that we're going to move on then to the next department.

02:31:40.600 --> 02:31:43.767
Thank you again, Rob. Thank you. Happy copying.

02:31:48.300 --> 02:31:49.133
All righty.

02:31:49.166 --> 02:31:52.166
Our next budget is social services general assistance.

02:31:52.400 --> 02:31:53.934
This budget's down

02:31:55.233 --> 02:31:58.967
over $360,000. That's a 19.3% decrease.

02:31:59.600 --> 02:32:03.066
The annual cost of the median residential taxpayer of this department is

02:32:03.066 --> 02:32:06.867
$21.94. And just so folks know, how we obtain that

02:32:06.934 --> 02:32:08.500
number is we take the costs

02:32:09.500 --> 02:32:12.333
for the department, we subtract non-property tax revenue.

02:32:12.400 --> 02:32:16.300
So in this case, the state reimbursed 70% of most of the GA

02:32:16.333 --> 02:32:20.100
costs, and then whatever is left over is what we need to raise from taxes.

02:32:20.133 --> 02:32:22.667
And so we figure out what that impact is on the average tax

02:32:22.734 --> 02:32:25.900
bill.This budget, there's big changes

02:32:25.967 --> 02:32:29.967
because demand is down. Now, it's down significantly over the

02:32:30.000 --> 02:32:32.066
past few years because there's

02:32:33.900 --> 02:32:37.800
less applications, there's less use, because we did have that spike during the

02:32:37.834 --> 02:32:41.667
pandemic, primarily due to the influx of folks being placed

02:32:41.700 --> 02:32:45.533
into the hotels, which were being used as de facto shelters.

02:32:46.467 --> 02:32:50.333
But before that happened, we were still seeing an increase

02:32:50.400 --> 02:32:54.066
in need. And so despite that spike, we're still on an upward

02:32:54.133 --> 02:32:58.000
trend. Even though we're down from the past few years, we're still on an upward

02:32:58.133 --> 02:33:00.867
trend over what we saw in the late '19s.

02:33:01.834 --> 02:33:05.400
But in reflection, or in reaction to the fact that

02:33:06.266 --> 02:33:08.000
we are seeing a decrease in

02:33:09.600 --> 02:33:13.066
requests, we have proposed eliminating a position

02:33:13.166 --> 02:33:17.100
mid-year. And that position is now actually vacant, so we're going

02:33:17.100 --> 02:33:20.667
to recommend this item be placed in the parking lot, and council can

02:33:20.700 --> 02:33:24.500
realize a full year's worth of savings versus the half-year that

02:33:24.533 --> 02:33:25.400
we've proposed.

02:33:26.467 --> 02:33:26.700
And

02:33:27.567 --> 02:33:31.200
again, we added two positions over the course of the past several years in this

02:33:31.266 --> 02:33:35.166
department, so we're still going to be up one position over where

02:33:35.200 --> 02:33:39.066
we were pre-COVID. And so that's still to deal with the increase in demand.

02:33:40.300 --> 02:33:44.266
But again, over the past few years, we've seen a decrease in needs for our food and

02:33:44.367 --> 02:33:45.367
rental assistance.

02:33:46.300 --> 02:33:49.433
And so those budgets are decreasing significantly as well.

02:33:50.166 --> 02:33:53.567
No capital requests here. And I'm going to turn it over to our Social Services

02:33:53.634 --> 02:33:56.800
Director, Chris Pupke, to talk more about this and his budget.

02:33:57.767 --> 02:33:59.967
Good evening. I'm Chris Pupke, Social Services Director.

02:34:00.266 --> 02:34:03.300
I don't have much to add. Scott just gave me a great intro.

02:34:03.767 --> 02:34:04.533
But effectively,

02:34:06.100 --> 02:34:09.667
numbers are down from the 2023 peak, but still

02:34:09.700 --> 02:34:13.700
above the historic numbers. We started to see an increase

02:34:13.767 --> 02:34:16.967
in 2021, '22, really.

02:34:18.467 --> 02:34:19.700
So we're still above those numbers.

02:34:20.600 --> 02:34:24.500
But our projections are based on actual usage, which is why we did

02:34:24.533 --> 02:34:28.367
a decrease for rent and food assistance, which those budget lines

02:34:28.400 --> 02:34:32.100
had been inflated to deal with that large

02:34:32.133 --> 02:34:35.900
increase in 2023. Those two specific types of assistance are things that

02:34:36.066 --> 02:34:39.066
folks at the hotels would be accessing, versus something like, say,

02:34:39.166 --> 02:34:41.967
electric assistance, which is for

02:34:43.000 --> 02:34:43.467
renters.

02:34:45.567 --> 02:34:49.533
So that's why we're kind of just getting those numbers down to more in line with

02:34:49.567 --> 02:34:50.700
what we're using at the moment.

02:34:51.667 --> 02:34:52.133
And

02:34:54.567 --> 02:34:56.100
I do just want to add that

02:34:57.133 --> 02:35:00.133
despite a decrease in the applications,

02:35:00.200 --> 02:35:03.967
this is how I get those numbers. Those are the numbers that we report to Department

02:35:04.000 --> 02:35:07.467
of Health and Human Services for reimbursement on a monthly basis.

02:35:07.500 --> 02:35:08.900
Those are unduplicated, so

02:35:10.200 --> 02:35:12.700
we will sometimes have to meet with somebody more than once a month.

02:35:13.100 --> 02:35:16.900
So just because the application numbers might be down, doesn't necessarily

02:35:16.967 --> 02:35:20.500
mean that it's just an equal decrease in actual

02:35:20.567 --> 02:35:23.800
workload. If somebody comes in and applies for assistance at the start of the month

02:35:23.800 --> 02:35:26.800
for their rent, and then comes back a few weeks later for their electric bill

02:35:26.967 --> 02:35:29.033
because they didn't have the bill at the time of the first appointment, that's two

02:35:29.066 --> 02:35:31.600
appointments, that's two meetings, takes about the same amount of time.

02:35:31.867 --> 02:35:35.533
But still, our applications are definitely looking like

02:35:35.834 --> 02:35:38.634
going in a good place. So I'm happy to answer any questions.

02:35:39.300 --> 02:35:40.700
Just want to make sure people understand, too.

02:35:40.767 --> 02:35:40.767
Thank you.

02:35:40.767 --> 02:35:42.266
We're not denying anyone.

02:35:42.367 --> 02:35:42.367
No.

02:35:42.400 --> 02:35:46.233
If they qualify for assistance, this budget doesn't say, "Sorry, we've run out of

02:35:46.300 --> 02:35:49.734
money," or, "We've cut that." Anyone who qualifies still gets assistance.

02:35:49.767 --> 02:35:52.900
What we're saying is that because the demand is not there, we don't need to budget

02:35:53.500 --> 02:35:57.433
as large of an amount. But this isn't reducing benefits we give to

02:35:57.467 --> 02:36:01.433
anyone, either if they qualify, they're getting less, or they qualify,

02:36:01.500 --> 02:36:03.066
but they're getting nothing. That's not the case.

02:36:04.133 --> 02:36:04.634
And if there's a-

02:36:04.700 --> 02:36:05.266
Just want to make sure that's clear

02:36:05.400 --> 02:36:09.100
... a huge unanticipated increase in demand, something catastrophic

02:36:09.133 --> 02:36:11.233
happens like global pandemic,

02:36:13.233 --> 02:36:15.900
we can't deny assistance if we exhaust our budget.

02:36:15.967 --> 02:36:19.867
So if I overspend on my food, the state says we

02:36:19.900 --> 02:36:21.100
still have to provide that assistance.

02:36:23.300 --> 02:36:26.000
Okay. Counselors, questions?

02:36:28.500 --> 02:36:31.700
I have one, and I apologize if it should be clear to me from this, but I always

02:36:31.700 --> 02:36:33.100
think it's a good idea to

02:36:34.200 --> 02:36:36.100
understand how much of the GA

02:36:37.567 --> 02:36:41.500
money in comes from the state and how much from South

02:36:41.533 --> 02:36:41.934
Portland.

02:36:42.166 --> 02:36:45.867
So the state reimburses 70% for the actual assistance side of it

02:36:47.767 --> 02:36:51.600
and doesn't provide any reimbursement for the administrative side.

02:36:52.834 --> 02:36:55.700
But the actual assistance we grant is reimbursed at a rate of 70%.

02:36:56.867 --> 02:36:58.166
Okay. And there's

02:36:59.266 --> 02:37:02.467
nothing that's going on, and that has been going on in Augusta that makes us think

02:37:02.467 --> 02:37:03.800
that's going to change?

02:37:04.967 --> 02:37:07.400
So there has been some talk of...

02:37:08.433 --> 02:37:11.433
There's always requests to increase. We're always pushing for that.

02:37:11.667 --> 02:37:11.767
Mm-hmm.

02:37:12.867 --> 02:37:16.600
There has been some talk or some proposed legislation, I guess,

02:37:16.767 --> 02:37:18.600
to potentially increase that amount.

02:37:19.166 --> 02:37:21.367
