04:25:20.767 --> 04:25:24.333
line. So basically, if a boiler goes down in a building, an air handler,

04:25:25.166 --> 04:25:29.066
an electrical issue, major electrical issue, basically these funds

04:25:29.100 --> 04:25:32.834
in this line, we try to put it out to all those repairs in the

04:25:32.900 --> 04:25:33.233
city.

04:25:34.166 --> 04:25:35.700
This year, if you look,

04:25:36.600 --> 04:25:40.400
we had budgeted 144. The reason I did that

04:25:40.500 --> 04:25:43.634
was because we started putting so much stuff in the preventive maintenance.

04:25:44.300 --> 04:25:44.300
In

04:25:45.767 --> 04:25:49.467
a newer building, typically you start doing preventative maintenance, it cuts

04:25:49.500 --> 04:25:51.066
back on your reactive maintenance.

04:25:51.300 --> 04:25:54.200
So those emergencies don't happen because you're doing preventative work.

04:25:55.867 --> 04:25:59.667
So I, over the last couple of years, have increased that preventative maintenance

04:25:59.767 --> 04:26:02.533
line, such as we're doing a little bit this year as well.

04:26:02.600 --> 04:26:06.500
And my theory was that the reactive maintenance would drop.

04:26:06.567 --> 04:26:10.400
Well, I didn't take into consideration police station, fire station,

04:26:10.567 --> 04:26:12.467
and some of these older buildings, City Hall.

04:26:13.767 --> 04:26:17.600
The beginning of this fiscal year, I had six or seven

04:26:17.700 --> 04:26:21.200
$10,000 jobs. I lost a boiler over at PD.

04:26:21.300 --> 04:26:24.133
I lost a rooftop unit. We had a condensing coil go.

04:26:24.533 --> 04:26:28.066
All those jobs were like $10,000. So it started eating up our

04:26:28.100 --> 04:26:29.700
